# ---- existing domain expansions: queries ----
new_lessons += [
 L("queries","SELECT essentials: projection, DISTINCT, TOP/LIMIT",
  ["Projection: SELECT lists exactly the columns the consumer needs. SELECT * is fine for exploration but a liability in production code (schema changes break consumers, extra I/O).",
   "DISTINCT removes duplicate rows across the whole select list, not one column \u2014 DISTINCT a, b dedupes (a,b) pairs.",
   "TOP (T-SQL) / LIMIT (Postgres, MySQL) / FETCH FIRST cap rows; always pair with ORDER BY or the 'top N' is arbitrary.",
   "Column aliases rename output; table aliases shorten references and are required for self joins. Alias in ORDER BY works (runs after SELECT); alias in WHERE does not."],
  "SELECT DISTINCT region FROM customers ORDER BY region\nOFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 10 ROWS ONLY;",
  "Walkthrough: DISTINCT collapses duplicate regions; ORDER BY makes the result deterministic; FETCH caps at 10 rows. Without ORDER BY, which 10 rows you get is undefined.",
  ["SELECT * in application code and views","DISTINCT on one column expecting per-column dedupe","TOP/LIMIT without ORDER BY and assuming a stable result"]),
 L("queries","Filtering: WHERE, LIKE, BETWEEN, IN, and NULL",
  ["WHERE filters rows before any grouping or aggregation. Put the most selective, cheapest predicates first for readability; the optimizer reorders anyway.",
   "NULL is not a value: = NULL never matches \u2014 use IS NULL / IS NOT NULL. NOT IN with a NULL in the list returns zero rows (three-valued logic trap).",
   "LIKE patterns: % multi-char, _ single-char. Leading wildcards ('%term') defeat B-tree seeks; prefer full-text search for contains-searches.",
   "BETWEEN is inclusive on both ends; for dates prefer >= start AND < day-after-end to avoid time-component bugs."],
  "SELECT id, name FROM products\nWHERE discontinued IS NULL\n  AND (name LIKE 'Pro%' OR sku IN ('A-100','B-200'))\n  AND price BETWEEN 10 AND 50;",
  "Walkthrough: IS NULL handles the nullable flag correctly; LIKE 'Pro%' is sargable (seeks); BETWEEN 10 AND 50 includes both endpoints. Each predicate narrows the set before it reaches SELECT.",
  ["= NULL instead of IS NULL","NOT IN against a subquery that can return NULL","BETWEEN on datetimes missing end-of-day rows"]),
 L("queries","Sorting and pagination that survives production",
  ["ORDER BY is the only guarantee of row order \u2014 without it, the engine may return rows in any order, and that order can change between runs.",
   "Keyset pagination (WHERE id > @last ORDER BY id LIMIT n) is stable under concurrent inserts/deletes and O(1) on deep pages; OFFSET re-scans skipped rows every time.",
   "Tie-breakers matter: ORDER BY created_at alone is unstable for equal timestamps \u2014 append the PK (ORDER BY created_at, id) for deterministic pages.",
   "Sorts are expensive: ORDER BY on an unindexed expression forces a sort operator. Index the sort key or rethink whether the client needs server-side sorting."],
  "-- Keyset pagination: stable, fast on deep pages\nSELECT id, name FROM products\nWHERE id > @last_seen_id\nORDER BY id\nOFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 20 ROWS ONLY;",
  "Walkthrough: the WHERE clause seeks directly to the resume point via the PK index \u2014 no counting past 40,000 skipped rows. @last_seen_id comes from the previous page's final row.",
  ["OFFSET pagination on large, churning tables","ORDER BY a non-unique column with no tie-breaker","Assuming result order without ORDER BY because 'it always came back sorted'"]),
 L("queries","Set operations: UNION, EXCEPT, INTERSECT",
  ["UNION ALL concatenates result sets (fast, keeps duplicates); UNION dedupes (adds a sort/distinct \u2014 cost you pay only when you need it).",
   "Both sides must have the same number of columns with compatible types; column names come from the first query.",
   "EXCEPT returns rows in the first set not in the second; INTERSECT returns rows in both \u2014 NULLs are treated as equal for set-operation comparison (unlike =).",
   "Prefer UNION ALL + explicit dedupe logic when you know the sources are disjoint \u2014 you skip the hidden sort."],
  "-- Combine active and archived orders, then remove any already refunded\nSELECT id, total FROM orders\nUNION ALL\nSELECT id, total FROM orders_archive\nEXCEPT\nSELECT order_id, total FROM refunds;",
  "Walkthrough: UNION ALL is correct because an order lives in exactly one of the two tables (no dupes to remove, no wasted sort). EXCEPT then strips refunded orders. Names/types align positionally.",
  ["UNION where UNION ALL would do (paying for a sort you don't need)","Mismatched column counts/types across the set operator","Forgetting ORDER BY applies to the whole combined result, placed once at the end"]),
]

# ---- aggregations ----
new_lessons += [
 L("aggregations","GROUP BY and HAVING: the aggregation pipeline",
  ["GROUP BY collapses rows sharing key values into one row per group; every non-aggregated SELECT column must appear in GROUP BY (or be functionally dependent).",
   "HAVING filters groups after aggregation \u2014 it can reference aggregates; WHERE cannot. Decide 'row filter or group filter?' before writing.",
   "COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) skips NULLs; COUNT(DISTINCT col) dedupes first. Mixing them up is a classic wrong-answer generator.",
   "Grouping on expressions is fine \u2014 GROUP BY YEAR(order_date) \u2014 but the expression must match the SELECT list (or use the alias in dialects that allow it in GROUP BY)."],
  "SELECT region, COUNT(*) AS customers, AVG(order_total) AS avg_order\nFROM customers\nWHERE active = 1\nGROUP BY region\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 100\nORDER BY avg_order DESC;",
  "Walkthrough: WHERE drops inactive customers first (cheaper), GROUP BY builds per-region buckets, HAVING keeps only regions with 100+ customers, SELECT computes the aggregates, ORDER BY ranks. Each clause sees the output of the previous one.",
  ["Putting aggregate conditions in WHERE","COUNT(col) when you meant COUNT(*) and silently dropping NULL rows","Selecting a non-grouped, non-aggregated column"]),
 L("aggregations","Aggregate functions and NULL semantics",
  ["All aggregates except COUNT(*) ignore NULLs: AVG of (10, NULL) is 10, not 5. Decide whether NULLs should be excluded or coalesced before aggregating.",
   "MIN/MAX work on strings and dates too \u2014 handy for 'first/last' per group without a window function.",
   "SUM of an empty set is NULL, COUNT of an empty set is 0 \u2014 guard dashboards with COALESCE(SUM(x),0).",
   "Aggregates compose with CASE for conditional aggregation: one pass over the data produces many metrics."],
  "SELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS total,\n  COUNT(email) AS with_email,\n  COALESCE(SUM(amount),0) AS revenue,\n  MIN(created_at) AS first_seen\nFROM customers;",
  "Walkthrough: COUNT(email) skips NULL emails so with_email <= total; COALESCE turns an empty SUM into 0 for the dashboard; MIN(created_at) gives first-seen without sorting the whole table in app code.",
  ["Assuming AVG includes NULLs as zeros","Forgetting SUM over no rows is NULL, not 0","Running one query per metric instead of conditional aggregation"]),
 L("aggregations","Conditional aggregation with CASE",
  ["CASE inside an aggregate computes per-group breakdowns in a single scan: SUM(CASE WHEN status='paid' THEN amount END) is one pass, not N queries.",
   "The ELSE-less CASE returns NULL for non-matching rows, which aggregates ignore \u2014 exactly the behavior you want for conditional sums/counts.",
   "Pivot-style reports (one column per month/status/channel) are conditional aggregation; reserve PIVOT syntax for when the tooling demands it.",
   "Keep the CASE deterministic and sargable-friendly: compute on raw columns, not on function-wrapped ones, when the source query also filters."],
  "SELECT\n  DATEPART(year, order_date) AS yr,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN channel='web' THEN total ELSE 0 END) AS web_rev,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN channel='store' THEN total ELSE 0 END) AS store_rev,\n  COUNT(CASE WHEN total > 1000 THEN 1 END) AS big_orders\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY DATEPART(year, order_date);",
  "Walkthrough: one scan, one GROUP BY, four metrics. ELSE 0 vs no-ELSE: with ELSE 0 the SUM includes zeros (same result); for COUNT the no-ELSE version counts only matches. Choose deliberately.",
  ["One query per breakdown column (N scans)","CASE returning 0 vs NULL without thinking about COUNT vs SUM","Putting the CASE in WHERE instead of inside the aggregate"]),
 L("aggregations","ROLLUP, CUBE, and GROUPING SETS",
  ["GROUPING SETS lists exactly which group-by combinations to compute \u2014 subtotals and grand totals in one pass instead of UNION ALL of many queries.",
   "ROLLUP (a,b) gives (a,b), (a), () \u2014 hierarchical subtotals for drill-down reports. CUBE (a,b) gives all 2^n combinations.",
   "GROUPING(col) distinguishes a real NULL from a subtotal NULL in the output so reports can label 'All regions' correctly.",
   "Use these for reporting layers, not hot OLTP paths: they multiply aggregation work, so schedule them in batch/warehouse workloads."],
  "SELECT region, channel, SUM(total) AS rev, GROUPING(region) AS g_reg\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY ROLLUP(region, channel);",
  "Walkthrough: one scan yields per-(region,channel) rows, per-region subtotals (channel NULL, g_reg=0... actually GROUPING(channel)=1), and the grand total. The report layer turns g_reg/g_chan flags into 'All' labels.",
  ["UNION ALL of separate GROUP BY queries instead of ROLLUP","Misreading subtotal NULLs as data NULLs","Running CUBE on high-cardinality columns and exploding the result"]),
]
# ---- joins ----
new_lessons += [
 L("joins","INNER and OUTER joins: matching vs preserving",
  ["INNER JOIN keeps only matching pairs; LEFT JOIN keeps every left row, filling NULLs where nothing matched. Choose by the question: 'which X have Y' vs 'all X, with their Y if any'.",
   "RIGHT JOIN is LEFT JOIN with the tables swapped \u2014 standardize on LEFT for readability; FULL OUTER keeps non-matching rows from both sides (rare, mostly reconciliation).",
   "The ON clause defines the match; the WHERE clause filters the joined result. For outer joins this distinction changes results \u2014 the #1 join bug.",
   "Join on indexed, type-identical keys. Mismatched types (int vs varchar) force conversions and kill seek performance."],
  "SELECT c.name, o.id AS order_id\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id AND o.status = 'open'\nWHERE c.region = 'West';",
  "Walkthrough: the status predicate lives in ON so customers without open orders still appear (with NULL order_id). Move it to WHERE and the LEFT JOIN silently becomes an INNER JOIN \u2014 the classic bug.",
  ["Filtering the nullable side in WHERE, collapsing the outer join","RIGHT JOINs scattered through a query","Joining on mismatched types and wondering why it's slow"]),
 L("joins","Self joins and non-equi joins",
  ["A self join treats one table as two roles (employee/manager) via two aliases \u2014 mandatory technique for hierarchies stored as adjacency lists.",
   "Non-equi joins match on ranges/inequality: join events to the price tier whose range contains the amount, or pair each row with its predecessor.",
   "Range joins can explode cardinality: verify the join produces the expected row count (a tier table with overlapping ranges duplicates rows).",
   "For deep hierarchies prefer recursive CTEs over chained self joins; self joins shine at exactly one level (manager, previous row)."],
  "SELECT e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager\nFROM employees e\nLEFT JOIN employees m ON m.id = e.manager_id;",
  "Walkthrough: two aliases, one table. LEFT JOIN keeps the CEO (no manager) with a NULL manager name. The alias is what makes the single table play two roles.",
  ["Forgetting the second alias on a self join","Overlapping ranges in a non-equi join duplicating rows","Chaining 5 self joins instead of a recursive CTE"]),
 L("joins","Semi and anti joins: EXISTS, IN, and their NULL traps",
  ["Semi join ('which customers have orders'): EXISTS or IN. Anti join ('which customers have NO orders'): NOT EXISTS or LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL.",
   "NOT IN is dangerous: one NULL in the subquery makes the whole predicate UNKNOWN and returns zero rows. NOT EXISTS is NULL-safe \u2014 prefer it.",
   "EXISTS short-circuits on the first match and ignores the SELECT list (SELECT 1); the optimizer often turns it into an efficient semi-join operator.",
   "For 'top per group' or 'latest per key', window functions usually beat correlated EXISTS on both clarity and performance."],
  "-- Customers with no orders, NULL-safe\nSELECT c.id, c.name\nFROM customers c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id);",
  "Walkthrough: NOT EXISTS checks the correlated condition per customer and stops at the first order found. Even if orders.customer_id contains NULLs, the logic stays correct \u2014 NOT IN would return nothing.",
  ["NOT IN against a nullable subquery","SELECT * inside EXISTS (works, but signals misunderstanding)","Correlated subquery where a window function is cleaner"]),
 L("joins","Join order, cardinality, and performance",
  ["Logical joins are commutative to the optimizer, but cardinality mistakes are yours: joining through a many-to-many bridge without dedupe multiplies rows and inflates SUMs.",
   "Fan-out check: after joining, compare row counts against the driving table. Unexpected growth means a join key isn't unique on one side.",
   "Pre-aggregate before joining when you need per-key metrics: aggregate orders to one row per customer first, then join \u2014 avoids row multiplication entirely.",
   "The optimizer picks join algorithms (nested loops / hash / merge) from statistics; stale stats cause bad picks, so keep them updated on churning tables."],
  "-- Safe pattern: aggregate first, then join (no fan-out)\nSELECT c.name, s.total_spent\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN (SELECT customer_id, SUM(total) AS total_spent\n           FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id) s\n  ON s.customer_id = c.id;",
  "Walkthrough: the derived table collapses orders to one row per customer before the join, so customer rows never duplicate and total_spent is exact. Joining raw orders then summing would need DISTINCT gymnastics.",
  ["Joining then SUMming across a fan-out (inflated totals)","Assuming a join key is unique without checking","Blaming the join type when stale statistics picked a bad plan"]),
]

# ---- windows ----
new_lessons += [
 L("windows","Ranking: ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK",
  ["Window functions compute across a set of related rows without collapsing them \u2014 every input row survives, with a computed column attached.",
   "PARTITION BY divides rows into groups (like GROUP BY but non-collapsing); ORDER BY inside OVER defines the ranking order within each partition.",
   "ROW_NUMBER is unique even with ties; RANK leaves gaps after ties (1,2,2,4); DENSE_RANK has no gaps (1,2,2,3). Pick by the business rule, not habit.",
   "The #1 use: top-N per group \u2014 rank inside the partition, filter rn = 1 in an outer query (window functions can't appear in WHERE)."],
  "SELECT customer_id, id AS order_id, total,\n  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date DESC) AS rn\nFROM orders;",
  "Walkthrough: each customer's orders are numbered newest-first. Wrap this in a CTE and filter rn = 1 for 'latest order per customer' \u2014 one pass, no correlated subquery, correct with ties broken deterministically by adding id to the ORDER BY.",
  ["Using window functions in WHERE (not allowed \u2014 wrap in a subquery)","ROW_NUMBER when the spec needs RANK's tie behavior","Non-deterministic ORDER BY inside OVER (ties break randomly)"]),
 L("windows","LAG, LEAD, and frame clauses",
  ["LAG/LEAD peek at previous/next rows within the partition \u2014 ideal for deltas: today vs yesterday, current vs previous status.",
   "ROWS BETWEEN defines the frame physically (1 PRECEDING = the row above); RANGE defines it logically by value (peers with equal ORDER BY values all count).",
   "Default frame is RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW \u2014 with ties, RANGE includes all peers, which surprises people expecting one row.",
   "FIRST_VALUE/LAST_VALUE need explicit frames: LAST_VALUE with the default frame returns the current row, not the partition's last row."],
  "SELECT day, revenue,\n  LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day) AS prev_day,\n  revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day) AS delta\nFROM daily_revenue;",
  "Walkthrough: LAG fetches yesterday's revenue alongside today's; the delta is plain arithmetic. First row gets NULL delta \u2014 wrap in COALESCE if the report needs 0.",
  ["RANGE vs ROWS confusion with tied ORDER BY values","LAST_VALUE without an explicit frame","Forgetting LAG returns NULL at partition edges"]),
 L("windows","Running totals and moving averages",
  ["A running total is SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY date ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING) \u2014 cumulative without collapsing rows, perfect for burn-up charts.",
   "Moving averages use a sliding frame: AVG(x) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) for a 7-day average.",
   "ROWS (physical) vs RANGE (logical) matters at the edges: with duplicate dates, RANGE's 6 PRECEDING is value-based and can include more than 7 rows.",
   "Partition the cumulative metric when it resets per group: SUM(x) OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY day) restarts the running total per region."],
  "SELECT day,\n  SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING) AS running_total,\n  AVG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS avg_7d\nFROM daily_revenue;",
  "Walkthrough: the running total accumulates from the first row; the 7-day average slides. ROWS keeps the window exactly 7 physical rows even when dates repeat \u2014 use RANGE only when you want peer-inclusive logic.",
  ["Default frame surprises with LAST_VALUE/FIRST_VALUE","RANGE frames on non-unique ORDER BY keys","Computing running totals in app code row-by-row"]),
 L("windows","Top-N per group and deduplication patterns",
  ["Top-N per group = ROW_NUMBER() in a CTE + filter rn <= N. It handles ties deterministically when the ORDER BY is unique.",
   "Deduplication: ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY the-dedupe-key ORDER BY preferred-row-first) then DELETE/keep rn = 1.",
   "NTILE(n) buckets rows into n roughly equal groups \u2014 quartiles, deciles for cohort analysis; buckets differ by at most one row.",
   "Prefer one window pass over repeated TOP 1 correlated subqueries: the window version scans once and the plan is usually a single sort."],
  "-- Keep the newest row per (customer_id, email) duplicate key\nWITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n    PARTITION BY customer_id, email ORDER BY updated_at DESC) AS rn\n  FROM contacts)\nDELETE FROM ranked WHERE rn > 1;",
  "Walkthrough: duplicates share a partition; newest wins rn = 1; the DELETE targets the CTE and removes the losers. ORDER BY updated_at DESC encodes 'keep the freshest' as the tie-break rule.",
  ["Correlated TOP 1 subqueries instead of one window pass","Non-unique ORDER BY making 'which row survives' random","NTILE on tiny sets expecting exactly equal buckets"]),
]
# ---- tsql ----
new_lessons += [
 L("tsql","Variables, batches, and control flow",
  ["T-SQL batches are separated by GO (a client directive, not a statement). Variables live for one batch: DECLARE @x INT; SET @x = 5;",
   "SET assigns one variable; SELECT can assign several at once (SELECT @a = col1, @b = col2 FROM ...) \u2014 but silently keeps old values when no row matches.",
   "IF/ELSE, WHILE, and TRY/CATCH give procedural control, but reach for them only where set-based SQL can't express the logic (row-by-row is a last resort).",
   "PRINT is for debugging; RETURN exits a procedure with a status code; THROW re-raises errors with context."],
  "DECLARE @cutoff DATE = DATEADD(day, -90, GETDATE());\nIF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders WHERE order_date < @cutoff AND status='open')\n  PRINT 'Stale open orders exist';\nELSE\n  PRINT 'Queue is clean';",
  "Walkthrough: the variable computes the cutoff once; EXISTS short-circuits; IF/ELSE branches on it. Variables also make scripts re-runnable by changing one value at the top.",
  ["Using GO inside stored procedures (not allowed)","SELECT-assignment silently keeping stale values on no-match","WHILE loops where a single UPDATE would do"]),
 L("tsql","Stored procedures: parameters and safe patterns",
  ["Procedures encapsulate logic with parameters: CREATE PROCEDURE dbo.GetOrders @status VARCHAR(20), @since DATE AS ... \u2014 defaults make parameters optional.",
   "OUTPUT parameters return scalar values; result sets return rows \u2014 pick result sets for data, OUTPUT for status/counts.",
   "SET NOCOUNT ON suppresses 'N rows affected' chatter that confuses drivers and ORMs. Schema-qualify names (dbo.Proc) to avoid resolution bugs.",
   "Dynamic SQL inside procedures needs sp_executesql with parameters \u2014 never concatenate user input (injection), and parameterize for plan reuse."],
  "CREATE PROCEDURE dbo.OrdersByStatus\n  @status VARCHAR(20) = 'open',\n  @count INT OUTPUT\nAS\nBEGIN\n  SET NOCOUNT ON;\n  SELECT id, total FROM orders WHERE status = @status;\n  SET @count = @@ROWCOUNT;\nEND;",
  "Walkthrough: default makes @status optional; the result set streams the rows; @count returns the row count via @@ROWCOUNT captured immediately. Callers get data + metadata in one round trip.",
  ["Concatenating parameters into dynamic SQL strings","Forgetting SET NOCOUNT ON and breaking ORM mappings","Not schema-qualifying procedure names"]),
 L("tsql","Functions: scalar, inline table-valued, and their costs",
  ["Inline table-valued functions (single RETURN SELECT) are macros the optimizer can fold into the plan \u2014 usually safe and fast.",
   "Scalar functions and multi-statement TVFs are plan poison: scalar UDFs force row-by-row evaluation (pre-2019), multi-statement TVFs hide cardinality (estimated 1 row... actually 100 pre-2019).",
   "SQL Server 2019+ inlines eligible scalar UDFs, but keep functions simple and deterministic to stay inlinable.",
   "Rule of thumb: business logic that filters/transforms sets \u2192 inline TVF or view; per-row formatting \u2192 scalar function only if inlinable, else compute in the query."],
  "-- Inline TVF: optimizer-friendly\nCREATE FUNCTION dbo.fn_RecentOrders(@days INT)\nRETURNS TABLE\nAS RETURN (\n  SELECT id, total FROM orders\n  WHERE order_date >= DATEADD(day, -@days, GETDATE()));",
  "Walkthrough: single-statement RETURN makes it inline \u2014 the optimizer merges it into the calling query's plan as if you'd written the SELECT directly. Call it with SELECT * FROM dbo.fn_RecentOrders(30).",
  ["Scalar UDF in a WHERE clause over millions of rows","Multi-statement TVF in a hot join path","Assuming 2019+ inlining fixes every UDF (it has eligibility rules)"]),
 L("tsql","Error handling: TRY/CATCH and transaction safety",
  ["TRY/CATCH captures runtime errors: in CATCH, ERROR_MESSAGE()/ERROR_NUMBER() log diagnostics; THROW re-raises to the caller.",
   "Pair with transactions: BEGIN TRY > BEGIN TRAN > work > COMMIT; CATCH > IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK > THROW. Never leave a transaction open on error.",
   "SET XACT_ABORT ON makes most errors abort the batch and roll back \u2014 the safe default for procedures that write data.",
   "Validate before writing: check preconditions (row exists, status allows transition) and raise meaningful errors instead of letting constraints fire cryptically."],
  "BEGIN TRY\n  BEGIN TRAN;\n    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\n    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;\n  COMMIT;\nEND TRY\nBEGIN CATCH\n  IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK;\n  THROW;\nEND CATCH;",
  "Walkthrough: both updates succeed or neither does. On any error, CATCH rolls back the partial work and THROW preserves the original error for the caller \u2014 no silent half-transfers, no orphaned open transactions.",
  ["CATCH without ROLLBACK leaving transactions open","CATCH that swallows the error (no THROW, no log)","Forgetting XACT_ABORT ON for multi-statement writes"]),
]
# ---- performance ----
new_lessons += [
 L("performance","Reading execution plans: the 5-minute triage",
  ["Get the actual plan (not estimated) for the real parameter values \u2014 estimated plans lie when statistics or sniffed parameters differ.",
   "Read right-to-left, top-to-bottom: data flows from the rightmost operators. Highest-cost operators and thick arrows (many rows) are your suspects.",
   "Scans aren't automatically evil: a clustered index scan over most of a small table is fine. The red flags are scans where a seek was expected, and huge gaps between estimated and actual rows.",
   "Key lookups multiply cost per row: a nonclustered seek + lookup per row is fine for 100 rows, deadly for 10M. Covering indexes eliminate them."],
  "-- Capture the actual plan, then look for: scan vs seek,\n-- estimated vs actual rows, missing-index hints\nSET STATISTICS IO, TIME ON;\nSELECT name FROM customers WHERE region='West' AND active=1;",
  "Walkthrough: STATISTICS IO shows logical reads per table (the real cost metric); TIME shows CPU/elapsed. Compare reads before and after each tuning change \u2014 reads don't lie, duration can (cache warmth).",
  ["Tuning from the estimated plan with atypical parameters","Treating every scan as a bug","Optimizing duration without checking logical reads"]),
 L("performance","Sargability: writing predicates the engine can seek",
  ["Sargable predicates let the engine seek an index: WHERE order_date >= '2026-01-01'. Wrapping the column in a function (YEAR(order_date) = 2026) forces a scan.",
   "Rewrite function-wrapped predicates as ranges: DATEPART/month/year filters become >= first-day AND < first-of-next-month.",
   "Implicit conversions are silent scan-forcers: comparing a VARCHAR column to an INT literal converts the column (not the literal) \u2014 match types exactly.",
   "Leading-wildcard LIKE ('%term') can't seek a B-tree; keep the literal prefix sargable ('term%') or move to full-text indexing."],
  "-- Non-sargable vs sargable: same logic, different plan\n-- Bad:  WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2026          -- scan\n-- Good: WHERE order_date >= '2026-01-01'\n--         AND order_date <  '2027-01-01'        -- seek",
  "Walkthrough: the range form preserves the column bare so the engine can seek the date index directly to 2026's slice. Same rows, orders of magnitude fewer reads on large tables.",
  ["Functions on indexed columns in WHERE/JOIN","Implicit int/varchar conversions on join/filter columns","LIKE '%...%' expecting index seeks"]),
 L("performance","Statistics and cardinality: why the optimizer picks badly",
  ["The optimizer chooses plans from statistics (data distribution histograms). Stale stats \u2192 wrong row estimates \u2192 wrong plan (nested loops over 10M rows).",
   "Symptoms of stale stats: a plan that was fast is suddenly slow after bulk loads; estimated vs actual rows differ by orders of magnitude.",
   "Fix: UPDATE STATISTICS on churning tables; consider FULLSCAN for critical ones. Auto-update exists but its thresholds can lag on huge tables.",
   "Parameter sniffing is statistics-adjacent: the plan is compiled for the first parameter value seen; atypical later values reuse a bad plan. Mitigations: OPTION (RECOMPILE), OPTIMIZE FOR, or plan guides."],
  "-- Diagnose: compare estimated vs actual rows in the plan;\n-- refresh stats and re-test\nUPDATE STATISTICS orders WITH FULLSCAN;\n-- then re-run the query and compare logical reads",
  "Walkthrough: after bulk loads, histograms describe yesterday's data. FULLSCAN rebuilds them exactly; re-running shows whether the plan flips to the sane shape. If it does, schedule regular updates.",
  ["Rebuilding indexes to fix what is actually a stats problem","Assuming auto-update stats keeps up on billion-row tables","Adding hints before checking statistics freshness"]),
 L("performance","The query-tuning workflow: measure, hypothesize, verify",
  ["Baseline first: capture logical reads, CPU, elapsed, and the actual plan before changing anything. No baseline, no proof of improvement.",
   "Change one thing at a time: one index, one rewrite, one stats update \u2014 then re-measure. Compound changes make cause and effect unknowable.",
   "Prefer correctness-preserving changes in order: fix sargability and types (free), update stats (cheap), add/adjust indexes (moderate), rewrite query (careful), hints (last resort).",
   "Verify under production-like conditions: cold cache, representative parameters, concurrent load. A fix that only helps on a warm cache isn't a fix."],
  "-- Tuning loop template\n-- 1. SET STATISTICS IO, TIME ON; capture actual plan  (baseline)\n-- 2. Hypothesize: 'scan on orders.order_date \u2012 missing index'\n-- 3. CREATE INDEX ix_orders_date ON orders(order_date);\n-- 4. Re-run: compare reads + plan  (verify)\n-- 5. Keep or DROP based on evidence",
  "Walkthrough: the loop is the scientific method applied to queries. Document each iteration's reads; if step 4 doesn't improve, revert \u2014 unused indexes tax every write.",
  ["Changing three things and declaring victory","Tuning on a warm cache with one hand-picked parameter","Keeping an index that didn't help ('might help later')"]),
]

# ---- new topical domains: lessons ----
new_lessons += [
 L("indexing-design","B-tree indexes: how seeks actually work",
  ["A B-tree index is a sorted structure over key columns: seeks walk the tree to the first matching key then scan the leaf range \u2014 O(log n) to start, sequential after.",
   "The clustered index defines the table's physical order (one per table); nonclustered indexes are separate structures pointing back via the clustering key.",
   "Index keys should be narrow, stable, and ever-increasing where possible: wide keys bloat every nonclustered index (they carry the clustering key).",
   "Choose the clustered key deliberately: identity ints are the default for good reason; GUIDs fragment; wide strings waste space everywhere."],
  "CREATE INDEX ix_orders_customer_date\n  ON orders (customer_id, order_date);",
  "Walkthrough: seeks for a customer's orders land directly on that customer's key range, then read order_date in order \u2014 both the filter and a date sort are served by one structure.",
  ["Indexing every column individually instead of designing composites","GUID clustered keys on insert-heavy tables","Assuming an index helps without checking the plan"]),
 L("indexing-design","Composite index column order",
  ["Order by selectivity AND query pattern: equality columns first, then range/sort columns. (customer_id, order_date) serves 'customer + date range'; reversed, it serves neither well.",
   "Left-most prefix rule: an index on (a,b,c) serves queries on a, (a,b), (a,b,c) \u2014 but not on b alone or c alone.",
   "Put the most selective equality column first when queries always filter it; when queries vary, design for the dominant workload and verify with the plan.",
   "Sort direction matters for ORDER BY ... DESC or mixed directions: match the index key order to the query's sort to avoid a sort operator."],
  "-- Serves: WHERE customer_id=@c AND order_date BETWEEN ... ORDER BY order_date\nCREATE INDEX ix ON orders (customer_id, order_date DESC);",
  "Walkthrough: customer_id equality seeks to one customer's slice; order_date range narrows within it; DESC matches the ORDER BY so rows stream out pre-sorted. Flip the columns and the seek degrades.",
  ["Reversed column order vs the query pattern","One giant index 'covering everything' instead of targeted ones","Ignoring sort direction in the key definition"]),
 L("indexing-design","Covering indexes and INCLUDE",
  ["A covering index contains every column the query needs: the plan never touches the table (no key lookups) \u2014 the fastest possible access path.",
   "INCLUDE adds non-key columns to the leaf level: they don't affect key order or seekability but they cover SELECT lists. Prefer INCLUDE over widening keys.",
   "Covering is a trade: wider indexes cost more on every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE and use more memory. Cover hot read queries; don't cover everything.",
   "Filtered indexes (WHERE status='open') cover a subset: smaller, faster to maintain, perfect for queries that always filter the same predicate."],
  "CREATE INDEX ix_orders_cover\n  ON orders (customer_id, order_date)\n  INCLUDE (total, status);",
  "Walkthrough: a query selecting total/status for a customer+date range is fully served from the index leaf \u2014 zero table access. INCLUDE keeps keys narrow (seekable) while covering the SELECT list.",
  ["Putting every column in the key instead of INCLUDE","Covering indexes on write-heavy tables without measuring write cost","Missing filtered indexes for always-filtered hot queries"]),
 L("indexing-design","Sargability revisited: index-friendly predicates",
  ["An index only helps predicates the engine can seek: bare column on one side, compatible types, no leading wildcards, no function wrapping.",
   "OR across different columns often defeats seeks (two seeks + concat is possible but not guaranteed); UNION ALL of two seekable queries is the reliable rewrite.",
   "Careful with data types in parameters: a NVARCHAR parameter against a VARCHAR column converts the column \u2014 declare parameters with matching types.",
   "Computed/persisted columns can be indexed: persist the expression (e.g., UPPER(email)) and index it when queries always filter the computed form."],
  "-- Seekable rewrite of an OR across two indexed columns\nSELECT id FROM orders WHERE customer_id = @c\nUNION ALL\nSELECT id FROM orders WHERE sales_rep_id = @c;",
  "Walkthrough: each branch seeks its own index; UNION ALL concatenates without a dedupe sort (a customer is rarely also the rep; add dedupe only if overlap is possible). More reliable than hoping the optimizer builds a concat plan.",
  ["OR across columns expecting two seeks","NVARCHAR parameters vs VARCHAR columns (silent conversion)","Function-wrapped columns in JOIN predicates"]),
 L("indexing-design","Index maintenance: fragmentation and fill factor",
  ["Fragmentation (out-of-order pages) hurts range scans; page splits (inserts into full pages) cause it. Sequential keys (identity) barely fragment; random keys (GUIDs) fragment fast.",
   "REORGANIZE defragments lightly online; REBUILD recreates the index (can be online in Enterprise). Don't rebuild on a schedule blindly \u2014 act on measured fragmentation.",
   "FILLFACTOR reserves free space per page for future inserts: lower values reduce splits on random-insert indexes at the cost of larger indexes.",
   "Monitor with sys.dm_db_index_physical_stats; automate maintenance by fragmentation thresholds, not by calendar superstition."],
  "-- Inspect fragmentation before deciding\nSELECT index_id, avg_fragmentation_in_percent, page_count\nFROM sys.dm_db_index_physical_stats(DB_ID(), OBJECT_ID('orders'), NULL, NULL, 'LIMITED');",
  "Walkthrough: fragmentation % is only actionable with enough pages (small indexes: ignore). High fragmentation + many pages + scan-heavy workload \u2192 REBUILD; moderate \u2192 REORGANIZE. Measure, then act.",
  ["Nightly rebuilds of every index regardless of need","Rebuilding tiny indexes (wasted work, no benefit)","Ignoring FILLFACTOR on random-key insert-heavy tables"]),
 L("transactions-concurrency","ACID: what the database promises",
  ["Atomicity: all-or-nothing \u2014 a crashed mid-transfer transfer leaves no half-moves. Consistency: constraints hold before and after. Isolation: concurrent transactions don't see each other's partial work. Durability: committed = survives crashes.",
   "Transactions are the unit of correctness: wrap every multi-statement write that must succeed or fail together in BEGIN/COMMIT.",
   "Keep transactions short: the longer a transaction lives, the longer it holds locks and blocks others. Fetch user input before BEGIN, never during.",
   "Single statements are implicitly transactional \u2014 the danger zone is multi-statement business operations (debit + credit, order + inventory)."],
  "BEGIN TRAN;\n  UPDATE accounts SET balance -= 100 WHERE id = 1;\n  UPDATE accounts SET balance += 100 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;",
  "Walkthrough: both updates are one atomic unit. A crash between them rolls back the debit \u2014 money is never created or destroyed. Without the transaction, a crash means $100 vanishes.",
  ["Multi-statement writes without explicit transactions","Holding transactions open across user interaction","Assuming single statements need no thought (they're atomic, but business ops span statements)"]),
 L("transactions-concurrency","Isolation levels: choosing your consistency",
  ["READ COMMITTED (default): no dirty reads; but non-repeatable reads and phantoms are possible \u2014 fine for most OLTP, wrong for 'read then decide' logic.",
   "REPEATABLE READ: locks read rows till commit (no non-repeatable reads); phantoms still possible; higher blocking.",
   "SERIALIZABLE: full isolation via range locks \u2014 correct but deadlock-prone; reserve for critical sections (inventory decrement).",
   "SNAPSHOT: readers see a committed snapshot, writers don't block readers \u2014 great for reporting, but writers can hit update conflicts; enable version store first."],
  "-- Safe inventory decrement under concurrency\nSET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;\nBEGIN TRAN;\n  SELECT qty FROM inventory WHERE sku=@s;   -- range lock held\n  UPDATE inventory SET qty = qty - @n WHERE sku=@s AND qty >= @n;\nCOMMIT;",
  "Walkthrough: SERIALIZABLE holds the range lock from SELECT to COMMIT, so two concurrent checkouts can't both see qty=1 and both succeed. The AND qty >= @n guard makes oversell impossible even if logic races.",
  ["Default isolation for read-then-write business logic","SERIALIZABLE everywhere (deadlock storm)","SNAPSHOT without understanding update conflicts"]),
 L("transactions-concurrency","Locking, blocking, and deadlocks",
  ["Locks are how isolation is enforced: shared (reads), exclusive (writes), and their granularities (row/page/table). Blocking is normal; excessive blocking is a design smell.",
   "Deadlock = cycle: T1 locks A waits for B, T2 locks B waits for A. The engine picks a victim and rolls it back \u2014 your code must retry victims, not crash.",
   "Prevention: access tables in the same order everywhere, keep transactions short, use the lowest isolation that stays correct, prefer row-level operations.",
   "Diagnose with the deadlock graph (XML): it shows the exact resources and statements in the cycle \u2014 fix the access pattern, not the victim."],
  "-- Retry template for deadlock victims (error 1205)\nDECLARE @tries INT = 0;\nWHILE @tries < 3 BEGIN\n  BEGIN TRY BEGIN TRAN; /* ...work... */ COMMIT; BREAK; END TRY\n  BEGIN CATCH IF @@TRANCOUNT>0 ROLLBACK;\n    IF ERROR_NUMBER() <> 1205 THROW; SET @tries += 1; END CATCH\nEND;",
  "Walkthrough: only error 1205 (deadlock victim) retries; anything else re-throws immediately. Bounded retries prevent infinite loops. The real fix is still consistent access order \u2014 retries just make victims survivable.",
  ["No retry logic on deadlock victims","Fixing deadlocks by killing processes instead of access order","Escalating to table locks 'to be safe'"]),
 L("transactions-concurrency","Optimistic concurrency: rowversion patterns",
  ["Pessimistic locking blocks readers/writers; optimistic concurrency lets everyone proceed and checks at write time \u2014 ideal for low-contention edits (user profiles, documents).",
   "Pattern: read row + rowversion; on save, UPDATE ... WHERE id=@id AND rowversion=@rv; if @@ROWCOUNT=0, someone changed it \u2014 reload and ask the user to merge.",
   "rowversion is database-wide auto-incrementing binary \u2014 perfect for change detection, meaningless as a timestamp.",
   "Choose by contention: high-contention counters \u2192 atomic UPDATE (qty = qty - 1); low-contention user edits \u2192 optimistic check."],
  "-- Optimistic save: only writes if nobody touched the row\nUPDATE documents\nSET body = @body\nWHERE id = @id AND rowversion = @rv;\n-- @@ROWCOUNT = 0 \u2192 conflict: reload, show diff, let user merge",
  "Walkthrough: no locks held while the user edits (minutes). The WHERE clause is the entire concurrency control \u2014 last-writer-wins becomes explicit-conflict instead of silent overwrite.",
  ["Last-writer-wins silent overwrites on shared records","Holding locks while users think","Using datetime 'last modified' instead of rowversion for conflict checks"]),
 L("transactions-concurrency","Transaction design: batching and error strategy",
  ["Batch large writes: one giant transaction holds locks forever and balloons the log \u2014 commit in chunks (e.g., 5,000 rows) with a restartable watermark.",
   "Idempotent batches survive restarts: watermark on processed keys so a crashed batch resumes instead of duplicating.",
   "Savepoints allow partial rollback: SAVE TRAN sp1 before a risky step; ROLLBACK TRAN sp1 undoes just that step inside the larger transaction.",
   "Never catch-and-continue past a data error inside a transaction without understanding XACT_STATE: some errors doom the transaction (uncommittable) \u2014 check before proceeding."],
  "WHILE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM staging WHERE migrated = 0)\nBEGIN\n  BEGIN TRAN;\n    UPDATE TOP (5000) staging SET migrated = 1, ... WHERE migrated = 0;\n  COMMIT;\nEND;",
  "Walkthrough: 5,000-row chunks keep each transaction short (locks released, log reusable). The migrated flag is the watermark \u2014 crash mid-loop and the next run continues where it stopped, no duplicates.",
  ["One transaction for a million-row migration","No watermark: restart reprocesses everything","Continuing after an error that doomed the transaction"]),
 L("security-access","Least privilege: roles and the access model",
  ["Least privilege: every login gets only the permissions its job needs \u2014 no db_owner for applications, no direct table access when procedures suffice.",
   "Roles (not users) own permissions: create app_read, app_write roles, grant to roles, add logins to roles. People change; roles don't.",
   "Ownership chaining: a procedure owned by dbo accessing dbo tables needs no per-table grants for callers \u2014 grant EXECUTE only, and the procedure is the security boundary.",
   "Separate duties: the deployment login can alter schema; the app login can only execute procedures and read views. A compromised app can't drop tables."],
  "CREATE ROLE app_read;\nGRANT SELECT ON dbo.orders TO app_read;\nGRANT EXECUTE ON dbo.PlaceOrder TO app_write;\nALTER ROLE app_read ADD MEMBER svc_webapp;",
  "Walkthrough: permissions attach to roles, users attach to roles. Auditing one role definition beats auditing fifty user grants, and PlaceOrder's EXECUTE-only grant keeps the app off raw tables.",
  ["Application logins as db_owner 'to avoid permission errors'","Granting permissions directly to users instead of roles","One super-login shared by app, jobs, and humans"]),
 L("security-access","GRANT, REVOKE, DENY: the permission verbs",
  ["GRANT gives, REVOKE removes a grant, DENY explicitly forbids \u2014 and DENY beats GRANT (a user in two roles, one denied, is denied).",
   "Permissions are securable-specific: SELECT on a view doesn't imply SELECT on its base tables (that's what ownership chaining is for).",
   "WITH GRANT OPTION lets grantees re-grant \u2014 powerful and dangerous; audit who holds it.",
   "Review effective permissions regularly: sys.database_permissions + role memberships show what a login can actually do, not what you think it can."],
  "-- Explicit deny overrides any role grant\nDENY DELETE ON dbo.orders TO reporting_role;\n-- reporting_role members cannot delete, even via another granted role",
  "Walkthrough: DENY is the emergency brake \u2014 it wins over all GRANTs. Use it sparingly and document why; a forgotten DENY causes baffling 'permission denied' tickets months later.",
  ["REVOKE when you meant DENY (and vice versa)","WITH GRANT OPTION scattered without inventory","Never reviewing effective permissions after role changes"]),
 L("security-access","SQL injection: the eternal vulnerability",
  ["Injection happens when input becomes code: string-concatenated SQL. The fix is structural, not filtering: parameterized queries separate code from data, always.",
   "Stored procedures help only if they use parameters internally \u2014 a procedure that concatenates parameters into EXEC() is still injectable.",
   "ORMs are not automatic immunity: raw query methods with f-string interpolation are just as vulnerable as hand-rolled SQL.",
   "Defense in depth: parameters first, least-privilege logins second (an injected query running as a read-only login can't drop tables), input validation third."],
  "-- Vulnerable vs safe (application code)\n-- BAD:  f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '{name}'\"\n-- GOOD: cursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = %s\", (name,))",
  "Walkthrough: the parameterized version sends the SQL shape and the value separately \u2014 the value can never become syntax. No escaping, no blocklists, no cleverness required.",
  ["Blocklists/escaping instead of parameters","Assuming stored procedures are automatically safe","Giving the web login db_owner so injection becomes catastrophic"]),
 L("security-access","Row-level security and data masking",
  ["Row-level security filters rows per user automatically: a predicate function (e.g., WHERE SalesRep = USER_NAME()) enforced by a security policy \u2014 the app can't forget the filter.",
   "Dynamic data masking hides sensitive columns from unprivileged users (emails show as a***@****) without changing stored data \u2014 masking is presentation, not encryption.",
   "Always Encrypted keeps sensitive columns encrypted such that even DBAs see ciphertext; the app holds the keys. Use for PII/credentials-grade data.",
   "Layer them: RLS for who sees which rows, masking for casual exposure, Always Encrypted for data even admins must not read."],
  "-- RLS: users only see their region's orders, enforced by the engine\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY region_policy\nADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_region_filter(region) ON dbo.orders;",
  "Walkthrough: every query against orders \u2014 ad hoc, ORM, report \u2014 automatically gets the region filter appended. No app code to forget, no bypass via a new tool.",
  ["Masking treated as encryption (DBAs still see masked, not encrypted)","RLS predicate functions that scan (keep them sargable)","Forgetting RLS applies to dbo/admin too unless excluded"]),
 L("security-access","Auditing: who did what, provably",
  ["Audits answer 'who changed this and when': SQL Server Audit / database audit specs capture logins, DDL, and DML on sensitive tables to tamper-evident logs.",
   "Capture at the right grain: failed logins (intrusion signal), privilege changes (escalation signal), DML on financial tables (fraud signal). Log everything and you'll drown.",
   "Protect the audit trail: write to a separate, append-only store with restricted access \u2014 an audit log the DBA can edit is theater.",
   "Complement with application-level audit tables (changed_by, changed_at, old/new values) for business-meaningful history the DB audit can't narrate."],
  "CREATE SERVER AUDIT app_audit TO FILE (FILEPATH = 'E:\\audit\\');\nCREATE DATABASE AUDIT SPECIFICATION fin_spec\nFOR SERVER AUDIT app_audit\nADD (UPDATE ON dbo.ledger BY dbo);",
  "Walkthrough: every UPDATE on the ledger table by anyone is captured with login, timestamp, and statement. The file target keeps it outside the database, harder to tamper with.",
  ["Auditing everything (noise buries signal)","Audit logs editable by the people being audited","No application-level history for business context"]),
 L("data-modeling","Normalization: 1NF through BCNF",
  ["1NF: atomic values, no repeating groups \u2014 one fact per cell. 2NF: every non-key attribute depends on the whole key (no partial dependencies). 3NF: no transitive dependencies (non-key attributes depend only on the key).",
   "BCNF: every determinant is a candidate key \u2014 stricter than 3NF, kills the remaining anomalies (e.g., overlapping candidate keys).",
   "Why it matters: normalized schemas make each fact live in exactly one place \u2014 updates can't contradict themselves, the classic anomaly killer.",
   "Normalize first, then denormalize deliberately where measured: normalization is the default; denormalization is an optimization with a maintenance cost."],
  "-- 3NF violation: customer name stored on every order (update anomaly)\n-- Fix: customers(id PK, name), orders(id PK, customer_id FK \u2192 customers)",
  "Walkthrough: with the name on every order row, renaming a customer means updating N rows \u2014 miss one and reports contradict. The FK design stores the name once; orders reference it.",
  ["Denormalizing before measuring a real problem","Repeating groups (phone1, phone2, phone3 columns)","Transitive dependencies hiding in 'convenient' columns"]),
 L("data-modeling","Keys and constraints: the schema as documentation",
  ["Primary keys: unique, non-null, stable \u2014 surrogate identity keys are the pragmatic default; natural keys (SSN, email) change and leak meaning.",
   "Foreign keys enforce referential integrity AND document relationships \u2014 the optimizer also uses them (trusted FKs enable better plans and simplifications).",
   "CHECK constraints encode business rules in the schema (balance >= 0, status IN (...)); UNIQUE prevents duplicates the app 'promises' to avoid; NOT NULL makes requiredness explicit.",
   "Name constraints explicitly (CK_orders_total_nonneg) \u2014 auto-generated names make deployments and error messages miserable."],
  "CREATE TABLE orders (\n  id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  customer_id INT NOT NULL REFERENCES customers(id),\n  total DECIMAL(12,2) NOT NULL CONSTRAINT CK_orders_total_nonneg CHECK (total >= 0),\n  status VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'open');",
  "Walkthrough: the PK, FK, CHECK, and NOT NULL together make illegal states unrepresentable \u2014 negative totals and orphan orders are rejected at the door, not discovered in reports.",
  ["No foreign keys ('the app enforces it')","Auto-generated constraint names","Natural keys as PKs that later change"]),
 L("data-modeling","ER modeling: from requirements to tables",
  ["Model entities (nouns), attributes, and relationships (verbs) first; tables come second. Cardinality (1:1, 1:N, M:N) determines the structure.",
   "M:N needs a junction table (students\u2014courses \u2192 enrollments); 1:N puts the FK on the many side; 1:1 is rare \u2014 usually a split for security or optional extension.",
   "Resolve optionality explicitly: can an order exist without a customer? The NULL/NOT NULL on the FK is a business decision, not a technical default.",
   "Validate the model with questions: 'show me all X with no Y' should be expressible; if a requirement needs procedural code to answer, the model is wrong."],
  "-- M:N resolved with a junction table carrying relationship attributes\nCREATE TABLE enrollments (\n  student_id INT REFERENCES students(id),\n  course_id INT REFERENCES courses(id),\n  enrolled_on DATE NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id));",
  "Walkthrough: the composite PK prevents duplicate enrollments; enrolled_on lives on the relationship where it belongs. Queries like 'students in course X' are simple joins \u2014 no arrays, no CSV columns.",
  ["M:N modeled as comma-separated columns","1:1 splits without a reason","Nullable FKs where the business says 'always'"]),
 L("data-modeling","Denormalization: when and how, deliberately",
  ["Denormalize for measured read pain: reporting queries joining 8 tables at dashboard refresh rates, not hypothetical slowness.",
   "Techniques: summary tables (pre-aggregated), cached counts (orders.order_count maintained by trigger/procedure), wide reporting tables fed by ETL.",
   "Every denormalized copy needs a refresh contract: trigger, scheduled job, or application write-through \u2014 and a reconciliation check proving it matches source.",
   "Document the tradeoff at the schema: a comment naming the source query it accelerates and the job that refreshes it. Future you will thank present you."],
  "-- Summary table with a refresh contract\nCREATE TABLE daily_revenue (day DATE PRIMARY KEY, revenue DECIMAL(14,2));\n-- refreshed nightly by job RefreshDailyRevenue; reconciled vs orders",
  "Walkthrough: dashboards read one narrow table instead of aggregating billions of order rows. The comment is the contract: what feeds it, when, and how drift is detected.",
  ["Denormalized copies with no refresh job","Triggers maintaining summaries on hot OLTP tables without measuring","Denormalizing before the normalized design is proven slow"]),
 L("data-modeling","Slowly changing dimensions: history done right",
  ["Type 1: overwrite (no history) \u2014 for corrections and attributes where history doesn't matter. Type 2: new row per change with effective dates \u2014 full history, the warehouse standard.",
   "Type 2 needs: surrogate key, effective_from/effective_to (or current flag), and ETL that closes the old row and opens the new one atomically.",
   "Type 3: keep previous value in an extra column \u2014 only for 'current vs previous' needs; anything deeper wants Type 2.",
   "Choose per attribute, not per table: a customer's name might be Type 1 while their segment is Type 2."],
  "-- Type 2: query 'as of' any date\nSELECT * FROM dim_customer\nWHERE customer_nk = @nk\n  AND @asof >= eff_from AND @asof < eff_to;",
  "Walkthrough: the date predicate picks the version that was current on @asof. Reports are reproducible for any point in time \u2014 auditors love this, Type 1 can never do it.",
  ["Type 1 where regulators require history","Overlapping effective ranges (broken ETL)","One SCD type for the whole table regardless of attribute needs"]),
 L("advanced-sql","Recursive CTEs: hierarchies and graphs",
  ["A recursive CTE has an anchor (base rows) and a recursive member (joins back to the CTE) \u2014 the engine iterates until no new rows. Perfect for org charts, BOMs, category trees.",
   "Always include a depth guard (level < 100) and cycle protection; runaway recursion from cyclic data is the classic production incident.",
   "MAXRECURSION (default 100) is a safety net, not a design tool \u2014 set it deliberately per query, 0 (unlimited) only with proven-acyclic data.",
   "Prefer recursive CTEs over while-loop tree walks: set-based, parallelizable, and the plan is inspectable."],
  "WITH org AS (\n  SELECT id, name, manager_id, 0 AS lvl FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT e.id, e.name, e.manager_id, o.lvl + 1\n  FROM employees e JOIN org o ON e.manager_id = o.id\n  WHERE o.lvl < 20)\nSELECT * FROM org OPTION (MAXRECURSION 50);",
  "Walkthrough: anchor grabs the CEO; recursion appends each level. The lvl guard plus MAXRECURSION 50 double-protects against cycles. Result: the whole tree in one statement.",
  ["No depth guard on user-maintained hierarchies","MAXRECURSION 0 'to be safe'","UNION instead of UNION ALL (dedupe cost per iteration)"]),
 L("advanced-sql","PIVOT, UNPIVOT, and conditional aggregation",
  ["PIVOT rotates rows to columns: one row per group, one column per category value. The IN-list must enumerate the values \u2014 dynamic pivots need dynamic SQL.",
   "UNPIVOT does the reverse (columns to rows), useful for normalizing wide survey/export tables.",
   "Conditional aggregation (SUM(CASE WHEN...)) is often clearer and portable across dialects \u2014 prefer it unless the tool demands PIVOT syntax.",
   "Dynamic pivot (unknown categories at design time): build the IN-list from DISTINCT values, execute with sp_executesql \u2014 and validate the generated SQL."],
  "SELECT region, [2024] AS y2024, [2025] AS y2025\nFROM (SELECT region, YEAR(d) AS y, total FROM sales) s\nPIVOT (SUM(total) FOR y IN ([2024],[2025])) p;",
  "Walkthrough: yearly totals become columns per region. The inner query projects exactly (group, pivot-col, value) \u2014 extra columns in the source silently become extra groupings, the classic PIVOT bug.",
  ["Extra columns in the PIVOT source creating phantom groups","Dynamic PIVOT via string concat without validation","PIVOT when conditional aggregation would be clearer"]),
 L("advanced-sql","JSON in SQL Server: storage and querying",
  ["Store JSON in NVARCHAR with ISJSON check constraints; SQL Server 2016+ queries it with JSON_VALUE (scalar), JSON_QUERY (object/array), OPENJSON (shred to rows).",
   "JSON_VALUE returns one scalar per path \u2014 index it via a persisted computed column when you filter on it often.",
   "OPENJSON with an explicit schema shreds documents into relational rows for joining \u2014 the bridge between document payloads and set logic.",
   "Don't model everything as JSON: query-heavy attributes belong in real columns; JSON is for flexible/sparse payloads, not for avoiding modeling."],
  "-- Shred order line-items JSON into rows and join\nSELECT o.id, li.sku, li.qty\nFROM orders o\nCROSS APPLY OPENJSON(o.items_json)\n  WITH (sku VARCHAR(20) '$.sku', qty INT '$.qty') li;",
  "Walkthrough: OPENJSON turns each document's array into rows with a typed schema; CROSS APPLY correlates per order. Result: document data participating in normal joins and aggregations.",
  ["JSON_VALUE in WHERE without a computed-column index","Storing everything as JSON to dodge schema design","OPENJSON without WITH (untyped, slow, error-prone)"]),
 L("advanced-sql","MERGE and upsert patterns",
  ["MERGE does INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE in one statement from a source to a target \u2014 the atomic upsert for ETL syncs.",
   "Match on a stable key; WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE, WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT. Add WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE for deletes carefully \u2014 it's a loaded gun on partial sources.",
   "MERGE has edge-case bugs (race conditions without HOLDLOCK); the safe pattern adds WITH (HOLDLOCK) on the target or uses separate UPDATE/INSERT in a transaction.",
   "OUTPUT clause captures what MERGE did ($action, inserted.*, deleted.*) \u2014 audit trail for every sync run."],
  "MERGE dbo.dim_product WITH (HOLDLOCK) AS t\nUSING staging_product AS s ON t.nk = s.nk\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.name = s.name\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (nk, name) VALUES (s.nk, s.name)\nOUTPUT $action, inserted.nk;",
  "Walkthrough: HOLDLOCK serializes the key range so concurrent MERGEs can't both insert the same key (the classic race). OUTPUT logs every action for the ETL audit.",
  ["MERGE without HOLDLOCK under concurrency","WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE DELETE on a partial feed","No OUTPUT audit on production syncs"]),
 L("advanced-sql","Gaps and islands: the classic analytic pattern",
  ["Gaps-and-islands groups consecutive sequences (dates, ids) into 'islands': subtract a row-number from the sequence value \u2014 constant difference = same island.",
   "Steps: order the sequence, compute ROW_NUMBER() over it, group by (value - rn). Each group is one contiguous island with MIN/MAX boundaries.",
   "Applications: consecutive active days, sessionization (events within N minutes), inventory coverage periods, streak detection.",
   "Sessionization variant: LAG to detect gaps > threshold, running SUM of gap flags as session id \u2014 same idea, time-based."],
  "-- Find consecutive login-day streaks per user\nWITH s AS (\n  SELECT user_id, login_date,\n    DATEADD(day, -ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date), login_date) AS grp\n  FROM logins)\nSELECT user_id, MIN(login_date) AS streak_start, MAX(login_date) AS streak_end,\n  COUNT(*) AS days FROM s GROUP BY user_id, grp;",
  "Walkthrough: for consecutive dates, login_date minus row-number is constant \u2014 that constant labels the island. Group by it and MIN/MAX give each streak's boundaries. One pass, no loops.",
  ["Looping in app code for streak detection","Forgetting to partition by the entity (user)","Date arithmetic that breaks across month boundaries"]),
]
# ---- capstone ----
new_lessons += [
 L("capstone-build","Capstone part 1: design the database",
  ["Project: build 'ShopDB' \u2014 customers, products, orders, line items \u2014 from requirements to a normalized, constrained schema. This is the artifact interviewers ask to whiteboard.",
   "Deliverable 1: ER sketch with cardinalities (customer 1:N orders, order M:N products via line_items). Deliverable 2: DDL with PKs, FKs, CHECKs, defaults, and named constraints.",
   "Apply the modeling domain: 3NF, surrogate keys, junction table for M:N, NOT NULL where the business says 'always'.",
   "Seed it with realistic volume (10k customers, 100k orders) \u2014 toy 10-row tables hide every performance lesson."],
  "-- Capstone DDL starter (extend to full schema)\nCREATE TABLE line_items (\n  order_id INT NOT NULL REFERENCES orders(id),\n  product_id INT NOT NULL REFERENCES products(id),\n  qty INT NOT NULL CONSTRAINT CK_li_qty_pos CHECK (qty > 0),\n  unit_price DECIMAL(12,2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (order_id, product_id));",
  "Walkthrough: the junction table carries relationship attributes (qty, price-at-purchase \u2014 price history matters). Composite PK prevents duplicate line items. Your job: complete customers/products/orders with the same rigor.",
  ["Starting queries before the schema is solid","10-row seed data hiding all real behavior","Unnamed constraints you'll regret at deploy time"]),
 L("capstone-build","Capstone part 2: query, index, and tune",
  ["Deliverable: a 10-query workload covering the interview greatest-hits \u2014 latest order per customer, running revenue, top products per category, monthly cohorts, streak detection.",
   "For each query: write it, capture the actual plan + logical reads, then design indexes (composite order, INCLUDE, filtered) and re-measure. Document before/after reads.",
   "Include one transaction design (checkout: decrement inventory + create order atomically) with isolation analysis, and one security pass (roles, parameterized access).",
   "Grading rubric: every query sargable, every hot path covered by an index, every multi-statement write transactional, plans showing seeks not scans."],
  "-- Capstone tuning log template\n-- Q3: top product per category\n-- baseline: 48,210 reads, clustered index scan\n-- fix: CREATE INDEX ix_p_cat_sales ON products(category_id, sales DESC)\n-- after: 312 reads, index seek. Kept: yes.",
  "Walkthrough: the log is the deliverable as much as the queries \u2014 it proves you can diagnose and verify, not just write SQL. Interviewers light up at before/after numbers.",
  ["Declaring victory without before/after measurements","Indexing without checking the plan changed","Skipping the transaction/security deliverables"]),
 L("capstone-build","Capstone part 3: review, harden, present",
  ["Harden: run the security checklist \u2014 least-privilege roles, no dynamic SQL concat, RLS/masking where PII appears, audit spec on financial tables.",
   "Review: re-read every query for the classic traps \u2014 NOT IN with NULLs, outer-join filters in WHERE, fan-out SUMs, non-deterministic TOP. Fix and note each one found.",
   "Present: a 10-minute walkthrough \u2014 schema diagram, 3 hardest queries with plans, one tuning win with numbers, transaction design, security model. This is your interview story.",
   "Ship the repo: DDL, seed scripts, queries, tuning log, and a README narrating decisions. A portfolio artifact beats a certificate line."],
  "-- Final review query: find any fan-out risk in your own schema\nSELECT t.name AS table_name, COUNT(fk.name) AS fk_count\nFROM sys.tables t LEFT JOIN sys.foreign_keys fk ON fk.parent_object_id = t.object_id\nGROUP BY t.name;",
  "Walkthrough: inventory your relationships before presenting \u2014 every FK is a potential fan-out in aggregations. Knowing your schema's join graph cold is what makes the whiteboard session effortless.",
  ["Presenting syntax instead of decisions and tradeoffs","No README \u2014 the repo doesn't explain itself","Skipping the security pass 'because it's a demo'"]),
]

# ---- scenarios: existing domains +2 each ----
scenarios=[
 S("sql-null-vanish","The vanishing NULLs","queries",
  "A report of 'customers without email' returns zero rows, but you know un-emailed customers exist. The filter is WHERE email != 'x'.",
  ["NULL comparison semantics","Three-valued logic"],
  [["report.sql","SELECT id FROM customers WHERE email <> ''; -- 0 rows"],
   ["sample","id=7, email=NULL | id=8, email='' | id=9, email='a@b.c'"]],
  "How do you correctly find customers with no usable email?",
  ["WHERE email IS NULL OR email = '' \u2014 NULL never satisfies <>, so test it explicitly",
   "WHERE email <> '' is fine; the data must be wrong",
   "WHERE ISNULL(email,'') <> '' works but hides intent; use COALESCE in the app instead",
   "WHERE email = NULL OR email = ''"],
  "The NULL comparison never returns true: NULL <> '' evaluates to UNKNOWN, which WHERE discards. Choice 2 is wrong because the data is fine \u2014 the predicate is the bug. Choice 3's ISNULL works but the explicit IS NULL version states the two cases (unknown vs empty) clearly. Choice 4 uses = NULL, which is never true. Correct: test NULL explicitly alongside the empty-string check.",
  hint="What does NULL <> '' evaluate to \u2014 true, false, or something else?",mins=5),
 S("sql-union-dedupe","Duplicate rows in UNION vs UNION ALL","queries",
  "Two regional sales feeds are combined with UNION, but finance reports the total row count is lower than the sum of the two feeds.",
  ["UNION vs UNION ALL semantics","Duplicate handling"],
  [["combine.sql","SELECT * FROM sales_east UNION SELECT * FROM sales_west;"],
   ["counts","east=120,000 rows, west=95,000 rows, combined=203,000 rows"]],
  "What explains the missing 12,000 rows, and what should change?",
  ["UNION dedupes identical rows across both feeds \u2014 12,000 rows exist in both; use UNION ALL if cross-region duplicates are legitimate",
   "UNION has a row limit; switch to UNION ALL to raise it",
   "The feeds overlap in time; add a date filter to each side",
   "UNION sorts and drops the last page; add ORDER BY to recover them"],
  "UNION performs an implicit DISTINCT over the combined set, so rows present in both feeds collapse into one \u2014 the 12,000 'missing' rows are exact duplicates across regions. Choice 2 is wrong: UNION has no row limit. Choice 3 misdiagnoses; a date filter doesn't restore deduped rows. Choice 4 invents behavior \u2014 UNION never drops pages. If the business expects both feeds' rows counted separately (e.g., each region's sales counted), UNION ALL is correct; if duplicates are data errors, keep UNION and fix the feeds.",
  hint="What does UNION do that UNION ALL does not?",mins=5),
 S("sql-having-where","HAVING vs WHERE: the misplaced filter","aggregations",
  "A query computing average order value per region for 'regions with 100+ orders' fails: the aggregate condition was placed in WHERE and SQL Server rejects it.",
  ["WHERE vs HAVING","Aggregation pipeline"],
  [["bad.sql","SELECT region, AVG(total) FROM orders WHERE COUNT(*) > 100 GROUP BY region;"],
   ["error","An aggregate may not appear in the WHERE clause"]],
  "What is the correct fix?",
  ["Move the aggregate predicate to HAVING: GROUP BY region HAVING COUNT(*) > 100",
   "Wrap the query and filter on the alias in an outer WHERE",
   "Use a subquery in WHERE comparing against a scalar aggregate",
   "Change COUNT(*) to SUM(1) so it is allowed in WHERE"],
  "WHERE filters rows before grouping, so aggregates don't exist yet at WHERE time \u2014 the engine is right to reject it. HAVING filters groups after aggregation, which is exactly 'regions with 100+ orders'. Choice 2 works but is needlessly convoluted. Choice 3 is wrong-shaped for a per-group condition. Choice 4 is nonsense: SUM(1) is equally an aggregate. Correct: HAVING COUNT(*) > 100.",
  hint="At which pipeline stage do aggregates come into existence?",mins=5),
 S("sql-conditional-count","Counting paid vs refunded in one pass","aggregations",
  "A dashboard needs per-day counts of paid, refunded, and disputed orders. The current code runs three separate queries.",
  ["Conditional aggregation","Single-pass metrics"],
  [["current","SELECT day, COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE status='paid' GROUP BY day; -- x3, one per status"],
   ["perf","orders: 40M rows; three full scans per refresh"]],
  "What is the best rewrite?",
  ["One GROUP BY with conditional counts: COUNT(CASE WHEN status='paid' THEN 1 END) per status",
   "UNION ALL the three queries \u2014 the optimizer merges them",
   "Add an index on (day, status) and keep three queries",
   "PIVOT with dynamic SQL for each status"],
  "Conditional aggregation computes all three metrics in a single scan and single GROUP BY: COUNT(CASE WHEN status='paid' THEN 1 END) counts only matching rows (non-matches yield NULL, ignored by COUNT). Choice 2 still scans three times. Choice 3's index helps each query but triples work remains. Choice 4 adds dynamic-SQL complexity for zero benefit over CASE. Correct: one pass with conditional aggregates.",
  hint="Can one GROUP BY produce three different counts?",mins=5),
 S("sql-leftjoin-where","The missing customers","joins",
  "A 'customers with their open orders' report uses LEFT JOIN but customers without orders are absent. The query filters o.status = 'open' in WHERE.",
  ["Outer join predicate placement","ON vs WHERE"],
  [["report.sql","SELECT c.name, o.id FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id=c.id WHERE o.status='open';"],
   ["symptom","customers with no orders missing from output"]],
  "What is the fix?",
  ["Move the predicate into ON: LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id=c.id AND o.status='open'",
   "Change to INNER JOIN \u2014 the WHERE already implies it",
   "Add OR o.status IS NULL to the WHERE clause",
   "Use a subquery filtering orders first, then LEFT JOIN it"],
  "WHERE filters the joined result: for customers with no orders, o.status is NULL, and NULL='open' is UNKNOWN \u2014 the row is discarded, silently converting the LEFT JOIN to an INNER JOIN. Moving the predicate to ON applies it during matching, so unmatched customers survive with NULLs. Choice 2 accepts the bug as intended behavior \u2014 wrong requirement. Choice 3 works but is fragile and obscures intent. Choice 4 also works (filtered derived table) but the ON-clause fix is the idiomatic one-liner. Correct: predicate in ON.",
  hint="For a customer with no orders, what is o.status after the join?",mins=6),
 S("sql-notin-null","NOT IN vs NOT EXISTS with NULLs","joins",
  "An anti-join 'customers with no orders' using NOT IN returns zero rows, though most customers never ordered. orders.customer_id is nullable.",
  ["Anti-join NULL trap","NOT EXISTS safety"],
  [["bad.sql","SELECT id FROM customers WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders);"],
   ["data","orders.customer_id contains some NULLs (guest checkouts)"]],
  "What is the correct fix?",
  ["Rewrite with NOT EXISTS: WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id)",
   "Add WHERE customer_id IS NOT NULL inside the NOT IN subquery",
   "Use LEFT JOIN ... WHERE o.customer_id IS NULL instead",
   "NOT IN is fine; rebuild the index on orders.customer_id"],
  "One NULL in the NOT IN list poisons every comparison: id <> NULL is UNKNOWN for all rows, so WHERE keeps nothing. NOT EXISTS is NULL-safe by construction (correlated equality with NULL never matches, which is the desired semantics). Choice 2 also fixes it and is legitimate, but NOT EXISTS is the idiomatic, optimizer-friendly standard. Choice 3 is equally correct but more verbose. Choice 4 is irrelevant \u2014 indexes don't change three-valued logic. Correct: NOT EXISTS.",
  hint="What does `5 NOT IN (1, 2, NULL)` evaluate to?",mins=6),
 S("sql-rank-ties","RANK ties and the off-by-one podium","windows",
  "A 'top 3 sales reps' leaderboard uses RANK() and sometimes shows 4 or 5 people. Management wants exactly 3 names.",
  ["RANK vs ROW_NUMBER tie behavior"],
  [["leaderboard.sql","SELECT rep, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY sales DESC) AS r FROM reps; -- outer filter r <= 3"],
   ["symptom","ties at rank 3 pull extra reps onto the podium"]],
  "How do you guarantee exactly 3 names?",
  ["Use ROW_NUMBER() instead of RANK() so ties get distinct numbers, with a deterministic tie-breaker in ORDER BY",
   "Keep RANK() and filter r < 3",
   "Use DENSE_RANK() \u2014 it never produces gaps",
   "Add DISTINCT to the outer query"],
  "RANK() assigns tied rows the same rank, so r <= 3 can match 5 rows when 3 reps tie for 3rd. ROW_NUMBER() gives every row a unique number \u2014 exactly 3 rows satisfy rn <= 3. The ORDER BY must be deterministic (add rep id as tie-breaker) or which tied rep survives is arbitrary. Choice 2 just moves the boundary problem to rank 2. Choice 3 is wrong: DENSE_RANK also shares ranks across ties. Choice 4 does nothing \u2014 rows are already distinct. Correct: ROW_NUMBER with deterministic ordering.",
  hint="Which function guarantees unique numbers even with ties?",mins=5),
 S("sql-frame-range-rows","Frame boundaries: ROWS vs RANGE","windows",
  "A 7-day moving average sometimes averages 9 or 10 rows. The frame is RANGE BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW over ORDER BY day.",
  ["ROWS vs RANGE frames"],
  [["ma.sql","AVG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day RANGE BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)"],
   ["data","day has duplicate dates (multiple batches per day)"]],
  "What is the fix?",
  ["Change RANGE to ROWS so the frame is exactly 7 physical rows regardless of duplicate dates",
   "Deduplicate the dates first with DISTINCT",
   "Change 6 PRECEDING to 7 PRECEDING",
   "Use LAG(revenue, 6) instead of the frame"],
  "RANGE is value-based: 6 PRECEDING means 'rows whose day is within 6 days', and all peers sharing a boundary date are included \u2014 with duplicate dates the frame holds more than 7 rows. ROWS is physical: exactly the 6 rows above plus current = 7 rows always. Choice 2 destroys the per-batch granularity the business may need. Choice 3 misdiagnoses the mechanism. Choice 4 computes a single lagged value, not an average. Correct: ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW.",
  hint="Is RANGE counting rows, or counting distinct date values?",mins=6),
 S("sql-scope-identity","OUTPUT vs SCOPE_IDENTITY in T-SQL","tsql",
  "An order-entry procedure inserts a header then line items. Under load, line items occasionally attach to the wrong order. It uses @@IDENTITY to get the new order id.",
  ["Identity capture scope","@@IDENTITY vs SCOPE_IDENTITY"],
  [["proc.sql","INSERT INTO orders ...; SET @oid = @@IDENTITY; INSERT INTO line_items ... @oid ..."],
   ["schema","orders has an AFTER INSERT trigger writing to audit_log (which has its own identity)"]],
  "What is the fix?",
  ["Use SCOPE_IDENTITY() (or the OUTPUT clause), which is unaffected by the trigger's identity insert",
   "Use IDENT_CURRENT('orders') \u2014 it reads the table directly",
   "Add WITH (NOLOCK) to the line_items insert",
   "Wrap both inserts in a transaction"],
  "@@IDENTITY returns the last identity generated in the session \u2014 including the trigger's audit_log insert, so @oid sometimes holds an audit id, not the order id. SCOPE_IDENTITY() returns the last identity in the current scope only (the orders insert). The OUTPUT clause (OUTPUT inserted.id) is equally correct and works for multi-row inserts. Choice 2 is worse: IDENT_CURRENT is session-independent and races under concurrency. Choice 3 is nonsense for correctness. Choice 4 doesn't fix which id was captured. Correct: SCOPE_IDENTITY() or OUTPUT.",
  hint="Whose identity did the trigger generate \u2014 and in which scope?",mins=6),
 S("sql-trycatch-tran","TRY/CATCH with a doomed transaction","tsql",
  "A procedure's CATCH block rolls back and re-throws, but callers report 'transaction count mismatch' errors after certain failures.",
  ["XACT_STATE","Doomed transactions"],
  [["proc.sql","BEGIN TRY BEGIN TRAN; ...; COMMIT; END TRY BEGIN CATCH ROLLBACK; THROW; END CATCH"],
   ["symptom","some errors leave @@TRANCOUNT inconsistent for the caller"]],
  "What is the robust pattern?",
  ["Check XACT_STATE() in CATCH: if -1 (doomed) roll back; only commit when XACT_STATE()=1; guard ROLLBACK with IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0",
   "Remove TRY/CATCH and let errors bubble naturally",
   "Add SET XACT_ABORT OFF so transactions survive errors",
   "Commit in the CATCH block to balance the count"],
  "Severe errors doom the transaction (XACT_STATE() = -1): it can only roll back, and a bare ROLLBACK without checking @@TRANCOUNT can itself error when the transaction already aborted, unbalancing the caller's count. The robust pattern: CATCH checks XACT_STATE \u2014 -1 means uncommittable (roll back), 1 means committable. Choice 2 abandons structured handling. Choice 3 is backwards: XACT_ABORT ON is the safe default for write procedures. Choice 4 commits partial work \u2014 a correctness disaster. Correct: XACT_STATE-guarded handling.",
  hint="Can every failed transaction still be committed?",mins=6),
 S("sql-implicit-conversion","The implicit conversion that killed the seek","performance",
  "An orders lookup by customer_id slowed 100x after an app change. The plan shows an index scan with a CONVERT_IMPLICIT warning on customer_id.",
  ["Implicit conversion","Sargability"],
  [["plan","CONVERT_IMPLICIT(int, orders.customer_id, 0) \u2014 index scan, 40M rows read"],
   ["change","app started sending customer_id as INT; column is VARCHAR(20)"]],
  "What is the fix?",
  ["Send the parameter as VARCHAR(20) matching the column type (or convert the parameter, never the column)",
   "Rebuild the index on customer_id",
   "Add OPTION (RECOMPILE) to the query",
   "Change the column to INT"],
  "When types mismatch, SQL Server converts the column (higher precedence wins), wrapping it in CONVERT_IMPLICIT \u2014 the predicate is no longer sargable, so the seek becomes a 40M-row scan. Converting the parameter side instead keeps the column bare and the seek intact. Choice 2 can't fix a conversion. Choice 3 addresses sniffing, not conversions. Choice 4 (alter the column) is a bigger migration \u2014 valid long-term, but the immediate correct fix is matching the parameter type. Correct: type-match the parameter.",
  hint="Which side of the comparison got wrapped in CONVERT \u2014 and which side should be converted instead?",mins=6),
 S("sql-parameter-sniffing","Parameter sniffing: fast for one value, slow for another","performance",
  "A reporting procedure runs in 2s for common @region values but 90s for one rare region. Same query, same plan shape, wildly different row counts.",
  ["Parameter sniffing","Plan reuse vs recompile"],
  [["symptom","plan compiled for @region='West' (2M rows, hash join) reused for @region='FarNorth' (200 rows)"],
   ["stats","statistics are current; estimated vs actual rows match the sniffed value"]],
  "What is the best first step?",
  ["Test OPTION (RECOMPILE): if per-execution compilation fixes it, the plan was the problem \u2014 then decide between recompile, OPTIMIZE FOR, or separate code paths",
   "Update statistics with FULLSCAN",
   "Add a covering index on region",
   "Increase the procedure's timeout"],
  "The plan was compiled and cached for the first-seen parameter value; the rare region reuses a hash-join plan built for 2M rows. OPTION (RECOMPILE) compiles per execution \u2014 if the rare region drops to 2s, sniffing is confirmed, and you then choose the durable fix (recompile for a rarely-run report, OPTIMIZE FOR UNKNOWN, or branch logic). Choice 2 is wrong: stats are current and estimates match the sniffed value. Choice 3 may help generally but doesn't address plan choice. Choice 4 masks the symptom. Correct: confirm with RECOMPILE, then pick the structural fix.",
  hint="What parameter value was the cached plan compiled for?",mins=6),
]

# ---- new topical domain scenarios ----
scenarios += [
 S("sql-idx-order","Composite index, wrong column order","indexing-design",
  "A query filters WHERE customer_id=@c AND order_date BETWEEN @a AND @b. The index is (order_date, customer_id) and the plan still scans.",
  ["Composite key order","Left-most prefix"],
  [["index","CREATE INDEX ix ON orders(order_date, customer_id);"],
   ["query","WHERE customer_id = @c AND order_date BETWEEN @a AND @b"],
   ["plan","index scan over the full date range, residual predicate on customer_id"]],
  "What index design serves this query?",
  ["(customer_id, order_date): equality first seeks to the customer's slice, then the date range narrows within it",
   "Keep (order_date, customer_id) \u2014 the optimizer should reorder the predicates",
   "Two single-column indexes; the optimizer will intersect them",
   "A clustered index on order_date"],
  "B-tree seeks need the left-most key: with (order_date, customer_id), the engine can only seek the date range (huge), then filter customer_id as a residual. Equality-first (customer_id, order_date) seeks exactly one customer's contiguous range, then applies the date range inside it \u2014 orders of magnitude fewer reads. Choice 2 is wrong: predicate order in SQL doesn't change index key order. Choice 3's index intersection is possible but far less efficient than one proper composite. Choice 4 doesn't fix the composite order problem. Correct: (customer_id, order_date).",
  hint="Which column's predicate is equality, and which is a range?",mins=6),
 S("sql-idx-cover","Key lookup storm on a hot query","indexing-design",
  "A dashboard query runs 50x/sec: seeks an index on orders(customer_id) then does 2M key lookups/sec for total and status.",
  ["Covering indexes","INCLUDE"],
  [["query","SELECT total, status FROM orders WHERE customer_id = @c;"],
   ["plan","index seek + key lookup (2M lookups/sec)"]],
  "What is the best fix?",
  ["Add INCLUDE (total, status): CREATE INDEX ... ON orders(customer_id) INCLUDE (total, status) \u2014 the query is covered, lookups vanish",
   "Widen the key: ON orders(customer_id, total, status)",
   "Make it a clustered index on customer_id",
   "Add OPTION (MAXDOP 8) to parallelize the lookups"],
  "The query needs total and status, which aren't in the index \u2014 every row pays a key lookup into the clustered index. INCLUDE puts them in the leaf without widening the key (key width affects tree depth and seek cost). Choice 2 works functionally but bloats keys unnecessarily. Choice 3 reorders the whole table for one query \u2014 absurd tradeoff. Choice 4 parallelizes waste instead of removing it. Correct: INCLUDE covering index.",
  hint="The seek is fine \u2014 what happens after each seek?",mins=6),
 S("sql-idx-filtered","Filtered index for the hot subset","indexing-design",
  "95% of order queries filter WHERE status='open' (5% of rows), but the index on (status, order_date) is huge and slow to maintain.",
  ["Filtered indexes"],
  [["workload","all hot queries: WHERE status='open' AND order_date > @d"],
   ["index","CREATE INDEX ix ON orders(status, order_date); \u2014 40M rows indexed"]],
  "What is the best design?",
  ["Filtered index: CREATE INDEX ix ON orders(order_date) WHERE status='open' \u2014 2M rows, smaller, faster maintains and seeks",
   "Partition the table by status",
   "Drop the status column from the index key",
   "A columnstore index on the whole table"],
  "Every hot query filters status='open', so indexing the other 38M rows is pure write overhead. A filtered index stores only matching rows \u2014 smaller, cheaper to maintain, and the optimizer matches queries whose predicate implies the filter. Choice 2 (partitioning) is heavy machinery for a 5% subset. Choice 3 keeps the full-size index. Choice 4 is for analytics scans, not selective seeks. Correct: filtered index.",
  hint="Do the cold 95% of rows need to be in this index at all?",mins=6),
 S("sql-idx-sarg","Function-wrapped date filter","indexing-design",
  "A yearly report WHERE YEAR(order_date)=2026 scans 40M rows despite an index on order_date.",
  ["Sargability"],
  [["query","SELECT ... FROM orders WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2026;"],
   ["plan","clustered index scan"]],
  "What is the fix?",
  ["Rewrite as a range: WHERE order_date >= '2026-01-01' AND order_date < '2027-01-01' \u2014 the column stays bare and the index seeks",
   "Create an index on YEAR(order_date)",
   "Add OPTION (RECOMPILE)",
   "Partition by year"],
  "YEAR(order_date) wraps the column in a function, so the engine must evaluate it per row \u2014 40M evaluations, no seek possible. The range rewrite expresses identical logic with the column bare, seeking exactly 2026's slice. Choice 2 (indexed computed column) also works but adds schema; the rewrite is free. Choice 3 is irrelevant. Choice 4 is overkill for a predicate bug. Correct: sargable range.",
  hint="Can the engine seek an index when the key column is inside a function?",mins=5),
 S("sql-idx-maint","Fragmentation: measure before rebuilding","indexing-design",
  "A nightly job rebuilds all indexes. Maintenance windows are shrinking and the job now overruns into business hours.",
  ["Index maintenance strategy"],
  [["job","ALTER INDEX ALL ON every_table REBUILD \u2014 nightly, 3 hours"],
   ["stats","70% of indexes < 1000 pages; fragmentation mostly < 10%"]],
  "What is the right change?",
  ["Rebuild/reorganize by measured thresholds (e.g., >30% + >1000 pages \u2192 rebuild; 5-30% \u2192 reorganize); skip the rest",
   "Switch to REORGANIZE for everything \u2014 it's always online",
   "Increase FILLFACTOR to 100 on all indexes",
   "Run the rebuilds in parallel with MAXDOP 0"],
  "Most of the work is waste: tiny indexes gain nothing from rebuilds, and low-fragmentation indexes don't need them. Threshold-driven maintenance does a fraction of the work with identical benefit. Choice 2 still processes every index nightly. Choice 3 is backwards \u2014 FILLFACTOR 100 leaves no room for inserts (more splits); and it's not the problem. Choice 4 speeds up waste. Correct: measure, then act on thresholds.",
  hint="What fraction of the rebuilt indexes actually needed it?",mins=6),
 S("sql-idx-guid","GUID clustered keys fragment","indexing-design",
  "A table with a NEWID() clustered PK shows 99% fragmentation hours after each rebuild, and inserts are slow.",
  ["Clustered key choice","Fragmentation causes"],
  [["schema","id UNIQUEIDENTIFIER DEFAULT NEWID() PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED"],
   ["pattern","heavy random inserts; page splits constant"]],
  "What is the best fix?",
  ["Change the clustered key to a sequential surrogate (IDENTITY) and make the GUID a nonclustered unique index if still needed",
   "Rebuild more often",
   "Set FILLFACTOR 50 on the clustered index",
   "Switch to NEWSEQUENTIALID() and keep the clustered GUID"],
  "Random GUIDs insert into random pages \u2014 every insert splits pages, fragmenting the index as fast as it's rebuilt. A sequential IDENTITY clustered key appends to the end (near-zero fragmentation); the GUID becomes a narrow nonclustered unique index. Choice 2 fights physics. Choice 3 halves page density permanently \u2014 a costly band-aid. Choice 4 (NEWSEQUENTIALID) genuinely helps and is the minimal change, but sequential IDs are still better understood, narrower, and friendlier to every nonclustered index (which all carry the clustering key). Correct: sequential clustered key.",
  hint="Where in the B-tree does each new random GUID land?",mins=6),
 S("sql-idx-missing","Reading the missing-index hint critically","indexing-design",
  "The plan suggests a missing index with 95% impact. A junior DBA wants to create every suggested index immediately.",
  ["Missing index DMVs","Index design judgment"],
  [["hint","CREATE INDEX ON orders(customer_id) INCLUDE (total) \u2014 impact 95%"],
   ["context","the suggested INCLUDE list grows with every new query variant"]],
  "What is the disciplined response?",
  ["Treat hints as per-query suggestions: consolidate across the workload into a few well-designed indexes instead of creating one per hint",
   "Create it exactly as suggested \u2014 95% impact is 95% impact",
   "Create it, then drop it next week if unused",
   "Ignore all missing-index hints; they're always wrong"],
  "Missing-index hints are narrow: each reflects one query's wish, and blindly creating them yields dozens of overlapping near-duplicate indexes that tax every write. The disciplined move is to aggregate suggestions across the workload and design a few composite/covering indexes serving many queries. Choice 2 creates index sprawl. Choice 3 is churn, not design. Choice 4 throws away a useful signal. Correct: consolidate, then create deliberately.",
  hint="Is the hint optimizing one query or your whole workload?",mins=6),
 S("sql-tran-checkout","Checkout transaction design","transactions-concurrency",
  "Checkout does: decrement inventory, create order, charge payment. A crash between steps has left inventory decremented with no order.",
  ["Atomic multi-statement writes"],
  [["code","three separate statements, no explicit transaction"],
   ["incident","crash after step 1 \u2192 phantom inventory loss"]],
  "What is the correct design?",
  ["Wrap all three in one transaction with TRY/CATCH + XACT_ABORT ON; validate preconditions first; keep it short",
   "Run each step in its own transaction for speed",
   "Decrement inventory last so crashes lose nothing",
   "Retry the whole checkout on any failure without a transaction"],
  "The three steps are one business atom: wrap in BEGIN/COMMIT so a crash rolls back all of them. TRY/CATCH + XACT_ABORT ON guarantees rollback on error; precondition checks (stock available, payment valid) happen before BEGIN so the transaction stays short. Choice 2 makes partial failure permanent. Choice 3 just moves the hole. Choice 4 without a transaction can double-charge. Correct: single atomic transaction.",
  hint="Which of the three steps may commit alone today?",mins=6),
 S("sql-tran-isolation","Phantom reads in a balance report","transactions-concurrency",
  "A funds-transfer validation reads the balance, checks sufficiency, then debits \u2014 under load, accounts go negative. Isolation is READ COMMITTED.",
  ["Isolation levels","Read-then-write races"],
  [["code","SELECT @b = balance ...; IF @b >= @amt UPDATE ... SET balance -= @amt;"],
   ["symptom","two concurrent transfers both see 100, both debit 80 \u2192 -60"]],
  "What is the correct fix?",
  ["Make the check-and-debit atomic: UPDATE ... SET balance -= @amt WHERE id=@id AND balance >= @amt, then check @@ROWCOUNT",
   "Raise isolation to SERIALIZABLE around the read",
   "Add UPDLOCK to the SELECT",
   "Run the check in a separate transaction"],
  "The race is between the read and the write: both sessions read 100 before either writes. The atomic UPDATE folds the check into the write itself \u2014 the WHERE clause is evaluated under the write lock, so only one session's predicate can be true. @@ROWCOUNT tells the loser to abort cleanly. Choice 2 works but serializes more than needed. Choice 3 (UPDLOCK) also works but holds locks longer. Choice 4 makes the race worse. Correct: single atomic conditional UPDATE.",
  hint="Can you express 'debit only if sufficient' as one statement?",mins=7),
 S("sql-tran-deadlock","Deadlock from inconsistent access order","transactions-concurrency",
  "Two procedures deadlock regularly: ProcA updates accounts then audit_log; ProcB updates audit_log then accounts. Deadlock graphs confirm the cycle.",
  ["Deadlock prevention","Access order"],
  [["graph","T1: accounts(X) waits audit_log; T2: audit_log(X) waits accounts"],
   ["code","victims retried, but deadlocks persist at 50/hour"]],
  "What is the durable fix?",
  ["Standardize access order: both procedures touch accounts before audit_log",
   "Increase the deadlock retry count",
   "Set DEADLOCK_PRIORITY HIGH on ProcA",
   "Add NOLOCK hints to the reads"],
  "The cycle exists because the two procedures acquire the same locks in opposite order. Retries (choice 2) make victims survivable but the 50/hour rate is pure wasted work; priority (choice 3) just picks the loser deterministically. NOLOCK (choice 4) doesn't apply to the conflicting writes and risks dirty reads. Eliminating the cycle by ordering access identically in both procedures removes the deadlock class entirely. Correct: consistent access order.",
  hint="Draw the lock-acquisition order of each procedure \u2014 where is the cycle?",mins=6),
 S("sql-tran-snapshot","Blocking: reports vs OLTP","transactions-concurrency",
  "A 10-minute financial report (READ COMMITTED) blocks order entry: writers wait on the report's shared locks.",
  ["SNAPSHOT isolation","Reader/writer blocking"],
  [["symptom","order INSERTs time out during the report run"],
   ["workload","report needs point-in-time consistency, not live data"]],
  "What is the best fix?",
  ["Enable SNAPSHOT isolation and run the report under it: readers get a committed snapshot, writers never block",
   "Run the report with NOLOCK",
   "Kill the report during business hours",
   "Raise the report to SERIALIZABLE"],
  "SNAPSHOT gives the report a transactionally consistent view as of its start without taking shared locks \u2014 writers proceed untouched. It needs the version store enabled (tempdb sizing is the operational cost). Choice 2 (NOLOCK) risks dirty reads and allocation-order anomalies in a financial report \u2014 unacceptable. Choice 3 is avoidance. Choice 4 maximizes blocking. Correct: SNAPSHOT for the reporting workload.",
  hint="Does the report need live rows, or a consistent as-of view?",mins=6),
 S("sql-tran-batch","Million-row migration in one transaction","transactions-concurrency",
  "A data migration updates 3M rows in one transaction. The log fills, the transaction rolls back after 4 hours, and the job restarts from zero.",
  ["Batching","Restartable workloads"],
  [["job","single BEGIN TRAN over 3M UPDATEs"],
   ["failure","log full \u2192 4 hours wasted, zero progress"]],
  "What is the correct design?",
  ["Chunk into batches (e.g., 5,000 rows) with a watermark column; each batch its own transaction; resume from the watermark",
   "Grow the log file and retry the single transaction",
   "Set the database to SIMPLE recovery during migration",
   "Run the migration single-threaded to reduce log pressure"],
  "Chunking bounds each transaction's log and lock footprint; the watermark (migrated flag / processed key range) makes the job idempotent and resumable \u2014 a crash loses one batch, not 4 hours. Choice 2 just delays the same failure. Choice 3 breaks the recovery chain and is often unacceptable. Choice 4 doesn't fix log growth. Correct: batched, watermarked, resumable.",
  hint="What unit of work can fail without losing everything?",mins=6),
 S("sql-tran-rowversion","Lost updates on shared records","transactions-concurrency",
  "Two admins edit the same product record; the second save silently overwrites the first. No concurrency control exists.",
  ["Optimistic concurrency","rowversion"],
  [["code","UPDATE products SET price=@p WHERE id=@id \u2014 last writer wins silently"],
   ["impact","pricing changes vanish without a trace"]],
  "What is the correct fix?",
  ["Optimistic concurrency: read rowversion with the row; UPDATE ... WHERE id=@id AND rowversion=@rv; on @@ROWCOUNT=0 reload and prompt to merge",
   "Lock the row with UPDLOCK while the admin edits",
   "Add a 'last_modified' datetime check",
   "Serialize all admin edits through a queue"],
  "The rowversion check makes the write conditional on 'nobody changed this since I read it' \u2014 conflicts become explicit instead of silent. No locks are held during the (long) human edit. Choice 2 holds locks for minutes \u2014 a blocking disaster. Choice 3 (datetime) has resolution and clock issues; rowversion is designed exactly for this. Choice 4 is heavy machinery for a solved problem. Correct: rowversion optimistic check.",
  hint="How can the UPDATE itself detect that the row changed?",mins=6),
 S("sql-tran-savepoint","Partial rollback with savepoints","transactions-concurrency",
  "An order import processes header + 50 lines per order in one transaction. One bad line aborts the entire order; the business wants good lines kept.",
  ["Savepoints"],
  [["code","single transaction; any line error \u2192 ROLLBACK all"],
   ["requirement","keep valid lines, quarantine bad ones, continue the import"]],
  "What is the correct pattern?",
  ["SAVEPOINT per line: SAVE TRAN line_sp before each line; on line error ROLLBACK TRAN line_sp, log the bad line, continue",
   "One transaction per line (50 commits per order)",
   "Skip TRY/CATCH and let the import fail fast",
   "Validate all lines before starting the transaction"],
  "Savepoints give sub-transaction granularity: rolling back to the savepoint undoes only the bad line, the outer transaction (and good lines) survive. The bad line is logged to a quarantine table for review. Choice 2 works but 50 commits per order is needless overhead and breaks the order's atomicity. Choice 3 abandons the requirement. Choice 4 is good practice AND should be done, but data errors still occur \u2014 savepoints handle the residual. Correct: savepoint per line.",
  hint="What transaction feature undoes part of the work but not all?",mins=6),
 S("sql-sec-leastpriv","App login with db_owner","security-access",
  "The web app's SQL login is db_owner 'so deployments don't hit permission errors'. A recent XSS bug has the team worried.",
  ["Least privilege","Role separation"],
  [["login","svc_webapp: db_owner"],
   ["threat","any SQL injection now runs as database owner"]],
  "What is the correct remediation?",
  ["Split duties: app login gets EXECUTE on procedures + SELECT on views only; a separate deploy login owns schema changes",
   "Keep db_owner but add a WAF in front",
   "Rename the login to obscure it",
   "Move permission checks into application code"],
  "Least privilege bounds the blast radius: an injected query running as a procedure-only login can only do what the procedures allow \u2014 no ad-hoc DROP TABLE, no direct writes to sensitive tables. Ownership chaining makes this ergonomic (grant EXECUTE, not table access). Choice 2's WAF is defense-in-depth, not a substitute. Choice 3 is security by obscurity. Choice 4 duplicates what the database enforces better. Correct: role-separated, minimal grants.",
  hint="If injection happens tonight, what can the login do?",mins=6),
 S("sql-sec-injection","Injection via dynamic search procedure","security-access",
  "A search procedure builds SQL by concatenating @term: EXEC('SELECT ... WHERE name LIKE ''%' + @term + '%'''). A pen test flagged it.",
  ["SQL injection","sp_executesql"],
  [["proc","EXEC('... LIKE ''%' + @term + '%''') -- injectable"],
   ["finding","penetration test: confirmed injectable"]],
  "What is the correct fix?",
  ["Parameterize with sp_executesql: pass @term as a parameter, concatenating only the wildcards in the parameter value",
   "Strip quotes and semicolons from @term first",
   "Grant the caller fewer permissions",
   "Move the LIKE to the application layer"],
  "sp_executesql with a real parameter keeps @term as data \u2014 it can never become syntax, no matter what it contains. The wildcards are added to the parameter value ('%' + @term + '%'), not to the SQL text. Choice 2 (blocklists) is the classic losing game. Choice 3 limits damage but leaves the hole. Choice 4 dodges the question. Correct: parameterize.",
  hint="Where does @term cross from data into code?",mins=6),
 S("sql-sec-rls","Multi-tenant data leak","security-access",
  "A SaaS app filters tenant rows in application code. A new reporting tool queries the database directly and exposes other tenants' data.",
  ["Row-level security"],
  [["app","WHERE tenant_id = @t in every app query"],
   ["leak","direct-query reporting tool has no tenant filter"]],
  "What is the correct fix?",
  ["Row-level security policy: FILTER PREDICATE on tenant_id = SESSION_CONTEXT value, enforced by the engine for every query path",
   "Add the tenant filter to the reporting tool's queries",
   "Create one database per tenant",
   "Revoke the reporting tool's access"],
  "Application-enforced filters only protect the paths that remember them \u2014 every new tool is a new chance to forget. RLS moves enforcement into the engine: the predicate applies to ad-hoc queries, ORMs, and reporting tools identically. Choice 2 repeats the original mistake on a new path. Choice 3 is a massive migration for a policy problem. Choice 4 kills the reporting use case. Correct: engine-enforced RLS.",
  hint="Which layer sees every query regardless of which tool sent it?",mins=6),
 S("sql-sec-masking","PII exposure in support tooling","security-access",
  "Support staff need order history but keep copying full customer emails and phone numbers into tickets.",
  ["Dynamic data masking"],
  [["need","support: order dates, totals, status \u2014 not contact PII"],
   ["exposure","PII pasted into ticket system weekly"]],
  "What is the correct control?",
  ["Dynamic data masking on email/phone for the support role: they see a***@**** while privileged roles see full values; stored data unchanged",
   "Encrypt the columns with Always Encrypted",
   "Tell support to stop copying PII",
   "Create a separate support database"],
  "Masking is presentation-layer protection: the same query returns masked values to the support role and full values to privileged roles, with zero app changes and zero stored-data changes. It directly stops the copy-paste exposure. Choice 2 (Always Encrypted) is stronger but heavier \u2014 and would block legitimate privileged reads without app key handling. Choice 3 is policy without enforcement. Choice 4 duplicates infrastructure. Correct: dynamic data masking by role.",
  hint="Can you hide the values from one role without changing what's stored?",mins=5),
 S("sql-sec-audit","Who changed the ledger?","security-access",
  "Finance found altered ledger rows. Nobody knows who changed them or when \u2014 no audit trail exists.",
  ["Auditing"],
  [["gap","zero record of DML on financial tables"],
   ["requirement","prove who/when/what for every ledger change"]],
  "What is the correct design?",
  ["Database audit specification capturing UPDATE/DELETE on ledger tables to a protected file target, plus an application audit table with old/new values",
   "Enable Change Data Capture only",
   "Add updated_by/updated_at columns to the ledger",
   "Restrict ledger access to one login"],
  "The audit spec gives tamper-evident, engine-level proof (login, timestamp, statement) written outside the database; the application audit table adds business context (old vs new values, reason codes) the engine can't narrate. Choice 2 (CDC) captures changes but is designed for ETL, not security audit, and is mutable by db owners. Choice 3 is forgeable by anyone with write access. Choice 4 reduces suspects but proves nothing. Correct: layered audit \u2014 engine spec + app history.",
  hint="What proves the change happened \u2014 even to someone who can edit the table?",mins=6),
 S("sql-sec-grant","DENY vs REVOKE confusion","security-access",
  "A contractor in two roles must be blocked from deleting orders. One role grants DELETE; an admin ran REVOKE and the contractor can still delete.",
  ["DENY semantics"],
  [["roles","contractor in data_entry (grants DELETE) and reporting (no DELETE)"],
   ["action","REVOKE DELETE ... FROM reporting \u2014 contractor still deletes"]],
  "What is the correct action?",
  ["DENY DELETE ON dbo.orders TO contractor (or to a role they're in): DENY overrides all GRANTs",
   "REVOKE DELETE from data_entry instead",
   "Remove the contractor from data_entry",
   "REVOKE is enough; wait for cache refresh"],
  "REVOKE only removes a grant from the named principal \u2014 the data_entry grant still stands, so the contractor still deletes. DENY is the explicit prohibition and wins over every GRANT from every role. Choice 2 would work but changes the role for all its members. Choice 3 also works but may break their legitimate duties. Choice 4 misunderstands: there's no cache, the grant genuinely remains. Correct: DENY.",
  hint="Does REVOKE remove grants held via other roles?",mins=5),
 S("sql-sec-proc-chain","Ownership chaining for safe access","security-access",
  "The app needs to place orders but must never touch the orders table directly. Direct grants feel wrong.",
  ["Ownership chaining","Stored procedure security boundary"],
  [["requirement","app places orders; no direct table writes"],
   ["objects","dbo.PlaceOrder procedure, dbo.orders table \u2014 same owner"]],
  "What is the correct design?",
  ["Grant the app EXECUTE on dbo.PlaceOrder only: ownership chaining lets the procedure write dbo.orders without any direct table grant",
   "Grant INSERT on orders and validate in the app",
   "Use an INSTEAD OF trigger for validation",
   "Give the app SELECT and have it call a web service for writes"],
  "When procedure and table share an owner, the engine checks only the EXECUTE permission \u2014 the procedure becomes a controlled gate: it validates, writes, and logs, while the app login holds zero direct table rights. Choice 2 hands the app raw write access. Choice 3 adds complexity without removing the grant problem. Choice 4 is architecture astronautics for a solved database feature. Correct: EXECUTE-only via ownership chaining.",
  hint="If procedure and table share an owner, which permission does the caller actually need?",mins=6),
 S("sql-mod-nf","Finding the normalization violation","data-modeling",
  "An orders table stores customer_name and customer_email on every row. Renames require updating thousands of rows and reports disagree.",
  ["3NF","Update anomalies"],
  [["schema","orders(id, customer_id, customer_name, customer_email, ...)"],
   ["symptom","rename customer \u2192 4,000 row updates; one missed \u2192 conflicting reports"]],
  "What is the correct fix?",
  ["Normalize: customers(id, name, email); orders keeps customer_id FK only \u2014 each fact stored once",
   "Add a trigger to sync the name across rows",
   "Create a view that picks the most common name",
   "Index customer_name for faster updates"],
  "customer_name depends on customer_id, not on the order \u2014 a transitive dependency violating 3NF. Storing it once in customers and referencing via FK makes renames a single-row update and contradictions impossible. Choice 2 (trigger sync) mechanizes the anomaly instead of removing it. Choice 3 papers over conflicting data. Choice 4 speeds up the wrong design. Correct: normalize to 3NF.",
  hint="What does customer_name actually depend on \u2014 the order, or the customer?",mins=6),
 S("sql-mod-mn","Modeling the M:N relationship","data-modeling",
  "Students enroll in courses. The current design has course1..course5 columns on the student table; course6 just arrived.",
  ["M:N junction tables","Repeating groups (1NF)"],
  [["schema","students(id, name, course1, course2, course3, course4, course5)"],
   ["pain","adding course6 = schema change; 'students in course X' needs 5 ORs"]],
  "What is the correct design?",
  ["Junction table enrollments(student_id, course_id, enrolled_on) with composite PK \u2014 M:N resolved properly",
   "Add course6..course10 columns now to get ahead",
   "Store course ids as a comma-separated list",
   "One row per student-course pair in the students table"],
  "Repeating groups violate 1NF and cap the relationship at an arbitrary number. A junction table models M:N natively: unlimited enrollments, enrolled_on lives on the relationship, the composite PK prevents duplicates, and queries are simple joins. Choice 2 repeats the mistake bigger. Choice 3 destroys queryability and integrity. Choice 4 duplicates student data (worse anomaly). Correct: junction table.",
  hint="What database structure represents 'many-to-many' without numbered columns?",mins=6),
 S("sql-mod-fk","The missing foreign key","data-modeling",
  "The app 'enforces' referential integrity. The warehouse is full of orphaned order lines pointing at deleted products.",
  ["Foreign keys","Declarative integrity"],
  [["gap","no FK constraints; 'the app checks'"],
   ["data","12,000 line_items with product_id matching no product"]],
  "What is the correct fix?",
  ["Add the FOREIGN KEY constraints (after cleaning orphans); let the engine enforce integrity for every writer, forever",
   "Add more app-side checks",
   "Schedule a nightly orphan-cleanup job",
   "Make product deletes soft deletes only"],
  "Application-enforced integrity protects only the paths that remember to check \u2014 bulk loads, new tools, and bugs all bypass it. Declarative FKs enforce for every writer including future ones, document the relationship, and help the optimizer. Choice 2 repeats the original failure mode. Choice 3 treats the symptom on a delay. Choice 4 is a fine policy but doesn't enforce anything by itself. Correct: real FOREIGN KEYs.",
  hint="Which writers does app-side checking protect against \u2014 and which does it miss?",mins=6),
 S("sql-mod-denorm","Denormalization with a refresh contract","data-modeling",
  "A dashboard aggregates 200M order rows on every refresh (45s). The team proposes storing order_count on the customer row, updated by the app.",
  ["Denormalization tradeoffs"],
  [["pain","45s dashboard refresh over 200M rows"],
   ["proposal","customer.order_count maintained by app code on each order"]],
  "What is the disciplined approach?",
  ["Denormalize deliberately: a summary table refreshed by a scheduled job with a reconciliation check \u2014 documented source, schedule, and drift detection",
   "Update order_count in app code on every order insert",
   "Add more indexes and hope the 45s drops",
   "Cache the dashboard in the browser"],
  "App-maintained counters drift the first time any write path forgets the increment (bulk loads, backfills, new services). A job-refreshed summary table has one writer, a schedule, and a reconciliation query proving it matches source \u2014 the refresh contract. Choice 2 is exactly the drift-prone pattern. Choice 3 won't fix a 200M-row aggregation. Choice 4 hides stale data without defining staleness. Correct: denormalize with an explicit refresh contract.",
  hint="How many code paths write orders \u2014 and will all of them remember the counter?",mins=6),
 S("sql-mod-scd2","Customer history for the auditors","data-modeling",
  "Regulators ask: 'what segment was customer X in on March 3rd?' The customer table overwrites segment in place \u2014 history is unknowable.",
  ["Slowly changing dimensions","Type 2"],
  [["schema","customers(id, name, segment) \u2014 Type 1 overwrite"],
   ["ask","point-in-time segment for any past date"]],
  "What is the correct design?",
  ["Type 2 SCD: new row per segment change with eff_from/eff_to; 'as of' queries filter the date range",
   "Add a segment_history audit table queried separately",
   "Restore from backups to answer each question",
   "Add previous_segment column (Type 3)"],
  "Type 2 makes history a first-class query: WHERE @asof BETWEEN eff_from AND eff_to returns the version current on any date, reproducibly. Choice 2 (separate audit table) works but splits the model and complicates every temporal query. Choice 3 is operationally absurd. Choice 4 (Type 3) answers only 'current vs previous', not arbitrary dates. Correct: Type 2 effective-dated rows.",
  hint="What schema lets you ask 'as of any date' with a single predicate?",mins=6),
 S("sql-mod-pk","Natural vs surrogate keys","data-modeling",
  "The schema uses email as the customer PRIMARY KEY. Users now want to change their email addresses, and email is referenced by 12 tables.",
  ["Surrogate keys","Key stability"],
  [["schema","customers(email PK); 12 FK tables reference email"],
   ["request","allow email changes"]],
  "What is the correct redesign?",
  ["Surrogate IDENTITY PK; email becomes UNIQUE NOT NULL; FKs reference the stable id \u2014 email changes touch one row",
   "Cascading updates on all 12 FKs",
   "Disallow email changes",
   "Composite PK (email, created_at)"],
  "Natural keys carry meaning, and meaning changes: every email change would cascade across 12 tables (locking, log, risk). A surrogate key never changes; the email becomes a unique attribute \u2014 renames are one-row updates. Choice 2 works mechanically but turns a trivial update into a 13-table distributed write. Choice 3 fights the business. Choice 4 keeps the instability. Correct: surrogate PK, unique natural key.",
  hint="What should happen \u2014 physically \u2014 when a user's email changes?",mins=6),
 S("sql-mod-optional","Optional relationships: NULL or not?","data-modeling",
  "Orders may have a coupon. The design stores coupon_code NULL on most rows; analysts keep tripping over NULL semantics in reports.",
  ["Optionality modeling"],
  [["schema","orders(..., coupon_code VARCHAR(20) NULL) \u2014 null on 92% of rows"],
   ["pain","analysts' GROUP BY coupon_code shows a giant NULL bucket; joins misbehave"]],
  "What is the better design conversation?",
  ["Keep the nullable FK if 'no coupon' is genuinely the common case, but expose analysts a view that translates NULL \u2192 'NO_COUPON' \u2014 or model redemptions as a separate 1:N table if coupons gain attributes",
   "Make coupon_code NOT NULL with a dummy 'NONE' coupon row",
   "Split into two tables: orders and coupon_orders",
   "Remove coupons from the model entirely"],
  "The NULL is honest (most orders have no coupon), but raw NULLs confuse consumers \u2014 a view translating NULL to 'NO_COUPON' keeps the model clean and reports sane. If coupons grow attributes (discount, campaign), a separate redemption table (order_id, coupon_id) is the proper 1:N. Choice 2's dummy row pollutes the coupon dimension. Choice 3 splits tables without a reason. Choice 4 deletes the business. Correct: nullable FK + consumer-friendly view, or a redemption table when attributes grow.",
  hint="Is the pain in the model, or in how consumers see the NULL?",mins=6),
 S("sql-adv-recursive","Bill of materials explosion","advanced-sql",
  "Manufacturing needs the full component tree under each assembly \u2014 assemblies contain sub-assemblies to arbitrary depth. The app loops in code.",
  ["Recursive CTEs"],
  [["table","parts(id, name, parent_assembly_id) \u2014 adjacency list, depth varies"],
   ["current","application recursion: N queries per assembly"]],
  "What is the correct query approach?",
  ["Recursive CTE: anchor on top assemblies, recursive member joins children, depth guard + MAXRECURSION",
   "Five chained self joins (max depth 5)",
   "A WHILE loop in T-SQL inserting into a temp table",
   "Flatten the hierarchy into the app and cache it"],
  "The recursive CTE expresses arbitrary-depth traversal in one set-based statement: anchor selects roots, the recursive member appends each level, and the lvl guard plus MAXRECURSION protect against cyclic data. Choice 2 breaks at depth 6. Choice 3 reinvents the CTE worse (row-by-row, no optimizer). Choice 4 moves the problem without solving it. Correct: recursive CTE.",
  hint="Which SQL construct iterates a join until no new rows appear?",mins=6),
 S("sql-adv-pivot","Monthly sales as columns","advanced-sql",
  "Finance wants one row per region with a column per month. The app currently pivots 12 query results in code.",
  ["PIVOT","Conditional aggregation"],
  [["need","rows: region; columns: Jan..Dec revenue"],
   ["current","12 queries + app-side pivot"]],
  "What is the best SQL approach?",
  ["Conditional aggregation: SUM(CASE WHEN MONTH(d)=1 THEN total END) AS Jan, ... \u2014 one pass, portable, explicit",
   "PIVOT operator with a hardcoded month list",
   "12 separate queries UNIONed",
   "Return rows and let the BI tool pivot"],
  "Conditional aggregation does the whole pivot in one scan with plain, portable SQL \u2014 each month is one expression, the logic is visible, and it works in every dialect. Choice 2 (PIVOT) also works but its syntax is dialect-specific and the mandatory IN-list is equally hardcoded. Choice 3 is 12 scans. Choice 4 is legitimate when a real BI tool exists, but the question asks for the SQL approach. Correct: conditional aggregation.",
  hint="Can a single GROUP BY produce twelve different sums?",mins=5),
 S("sql-adv-json","Querying the JSON payloads","advanced-sql",
  "Order line items arrive as a JSON array in one column. Analysts need sku/qty as rows for joining to the catalog.",
  ["OPENJSON","JSON shredding"],
  [["column","orders.items_json: [{\"sku\":\"A1\",\"qty\":2}, ...]"],
   ["need","rows of (order_id, sku, qty) joinable to products"]],
  "What is the correct approach?",
  ["CROSS APPLY OPENJSON(items_json) WITH (sku VARCHAR(20) '$.sku', qty INT '$.qty') \u2014 typed relational rows per order",
   "JSON_VALUE(items_json, '$[0].sku') for each array position",
   "Store the JSON pre-shredded by the app instead",
   "LIKE '%\"sku\":\"A1\"%' searches"],
  "OPENJSON with an explicit WITH schema shreds each document's array into typed rows, correlated per order via CROSS APPLY \u2014 the result joins and aggregates like any table. Choice 2 handles only fixed positions and explodes with variable-length arrays. Choice 3 is a fine architecture but doesn't answer the SQL question. Choice 4 is a correctness nightmare. Correct: OPENJSON shredding.",
  hint="Which function turns a JSON array into a rowset?",mins=6),
 S("sql-adv-merge","Nightly catalog sync","advanced-sql",
  "A nightly job syncs 500k products from a vendor feed: update changed, insert new. Two concurrent runs once double-inserted the same sku.",
  ["MERGE","Upsert races"],
  [["job","separate UPDATE then INSERT statements"],
   ["incident","concurrent runs both INSERTed sku X \u2014 duplicates"]],
  "What is the correct pattern?",
  ["MERGE ... WITH (HOLDLOCK) on the key, or the UPDATE/INSERT pair inside a SERIALIZABLE transaction \u2014 serialize the key range so only one session inserts",
   "Add a unique constraint and let one run fail",
   "Run the job single-threaded via a lock file",
   "DELETE all then INSERT all each night"],
  "The race is check-then-act: both sessions find no row, both insert. HOLDLOCK (serializable) on the MERGE target holds the key range through the statement, so the second session blocks until the first commits, then sees the row and updates. Choice 2 (unique constraint) prevents duplicates but turns the loser into an error \u2014 the sync is still broken. Choice 3 works operationally but serializes the whole job. Choice 4 is downtime-adjacent and loses history. Correct: serialized upsert.",
  hint="What happens between the 'check' and the 'act' under concurrency?",mins=6),
 S("sql-adv-gaps","Finding coverage gaps","advanced-sql",
  "Support contracts need contiguous coverage: find gaps in per-customer coverage periods (start/end dates) to flag uninsured days.",
  ["Gaps and islands"],
  [["table","coverage(customer_id, start_date, end_date) \u2014 multiple rows per customer"],
   ["need","date ranges with no coverage, per customer"]],
  "What is the correct technique?",
  ["Islands of coverage via grouped row-number arithmetic, then look for gaps between island boundaries (MAX(end) < next MIN(start))",
   "A calendar table LEFT JOINed to coverage, filtering NULLs",
   "Cursor over each customer's periods comparing dates",
   "DATEDIFF between consecutive rows with LAG"],
  "The islands technique collapses overlapping/contiguous periods per customer into continuous coverage blocks; gaps are where one island's end < the next island's start. Choice 2 (calendar join) also works and is arguably simpler to read \u2014 but on multi-year ranges per customer it's far more expensive; the islands approach is the set-based classic. Choice 3 is row-by-row. Choice 4's LAG only compares adjacent raw rows, missing overlaps that merge periods. Correct: islands then gap detection.",
  hint="First merge overlapping periods into continuous blocks \u2014 then what's a gap?",mins=7),
 S("sql-adv-topn","Top product per category, ties included","advanced-sql",
  "Marketing wants the top-selling product per category \u2014 including ties. ROW_NUMBER gives exactly one even when two tie for first.",
  ["RANK vs ROW_NUMBER","Top-N per group with ties"],
  [["query","ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY sales DESC) rn ... WHERE rn=1"],
   ["complaint","tied products arbitrarily excluded"]],
  "What is the correct fix?",
  ["RANK() instead of ROW_NUMBER(): tied top sellers share rank 1 and all satisfy r=1",
   "DENSE_RANK() instead of ROW_NUMBER()",
   "Remove the filter and sort by sales DESC",
   "Add a tie-breaker to the ROW_NUMBER ORDER BY"],
  "RANK() assigns equal values the same rank, so all tied leaders get rank 1 and pass the r=1 filter \u2014 exactly the 'ties included' requirement. Choice 2 (DENSE_RANK) behaves identically here (no gaps matter at rank 1), but RANK is the conventional choice. Choice 3 returns everything. Choice 4 (tie-breaker) does the opposite of what's asked \u2014 it arbitrarily excludes. Correct: RANK().",
  hint="Which ranking function gives ties the same number?",mins=5),
 S("sql-adv-running","Running total that resets monthly","advanced-sql",
  "Finance wants a running revenue total that resets each month \u2014 the current query accumulates across the whole year.",
  ["Window partitions","Running totals"],
  [["query","SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING)"],
   ["need","cumulative within each month, resetting on the 1st"]],
  "What is the fix?",
  ["PARTITION BY month: SUM(revenue) OVER (PARTITION BY YEAR(day), MONTH(day) ORDER BY day)",
   "Add a CASE resetting to 0 on the first of the month",
   "GROUP BY month first, then window over the groups",
   "Use a cursor resetting a variable monthly"],
  "PARTITION BY restarts the window frame for each month: the running total accumulates within the partition only. The ORDER BY inside keeps day order. Choice 2 can't work \u2014 window functions can't reference their own output. Choice 3 pre-aggregates (fine) but still needs the partition for the reset. Choice 4 is row-by-row. Correct: PARTITION BY year/month.",
  hint="What restarts a window function's frame?",mins=5),
]

